WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.970
for everyday life.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.860
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.930
of nNeurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.610
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.980
Today, my guest is Dr. Gina Poe.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.620
Dr. Gina Poe is a professor in
the Department of Integrative

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.860
Biology and Physiology at the
University of California Los

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:23.700
Angeles.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.280
Her laboratory and research
focuses on the relationship

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.800
between sleep and
learning, in particular

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.840
how specific patterns
of brain activity that

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.600
are present during
specific phases of sleep

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.510
impact our ability
to learn and remember

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.260
specific types of information.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.290
For instance, procedural
information-- that

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.680
is how to perform specific
cognitive or physical tasks,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.200
as well as encoding
of emotional memories

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.510
and discarding
emotional memories.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.570
Indeed, her research focuses
on how specific phases of sleep

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.090
can act as its own
form of trauma therapy,

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.600
discarding the emotional
tones of memories.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.340
In addition, her
laboratory focuses

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.830
on how specific phases
of sleep impact things

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.030
like the release
of growth hormone.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.000
Growth hormone, of course,
plays critical roles

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.240
in metabolism and tissue repair,
including brain tissue repair,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.840
and therefore has critical
roles in vitality and longevity.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.150
Today you will learn many
things about the relationship

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.060
between sleep, learning,
emotionality, and growth

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:18.750
hormone.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.300
One basic but very
important takeaway

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.580
that you'll learn about
today, which was news to me,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.040
is that it's not
just the duration

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.750
and depth of your
sleep that matter,

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.750
but actually getting to sleep
at relatively the same time

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.290
each night ensures that you get
adequate growth hormone release

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.100
in the first hours of sleep.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.390
In fact, if you require, let's
say, eight hours of sleep

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.390
per night, but you go
to sleep two hours later

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.000
than your typical bedtime
on any given night,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.540
you actually miss the window
for growth hormone release.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:49.650
That's right.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.400
Getting growth hormone
release in sleep,

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.060
which is absolutely critical
to our immediate and long term

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.070
health, is not a prerequisite
of getting sleep,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.570
even if we are
getting enough sleep.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.390
As Dr. Poe explains, there
are critical brain circuits

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.130
and endocrine, that
is hormone, circuits

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.220
that regulate not just the
duration and depth and quality

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.890
and timing of sleep,
but when we place

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.590
our bout of sleep, that is
when we go to sleep each night,

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.840
plus or minus about
a half hour or so,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.550
strongly dictates
whether or not we

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.820
will experience all the health
promoting, including mind

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.650
promoting, benefits of sleep.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.110
Today's episode covers
that and a lot more

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.135
in substantial detail.

00:02:23.135 --> 00:02:24.510
You will learn,
for instance, how

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.760
to use sleep in order to
optimize learning, as well

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.400
as forgetting, for those things
that you would like to forget.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.320
So during today's
episode, Dr. Gina Poe

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.290
shares critical information
about not just neuroscience,

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.120
but physiology and
the hormone systems

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.800
of the brain and
body that strongly

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.330
inform mental health, physical
health, and performance.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:41.790
So by the end of
today's episode,

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.940
you'll be far more informed
about sleep and how it works,

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.770
the different roles it
performs, and you'll

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.270
have several new
actionable steps

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.580
that you can take
in order to improve

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.040
your mental health, physical
health, and performance.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.932
Before we begin, I'd
like to emphasize

00:02:54.932 --> 00:02:57.390
that this podcast is separate
from my teaching and research

