00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:58.940
And now for my discussion
with Dr. Gina Poe.

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.920
Dr. Gina Poe, welcome.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:01.820
GINA POE: Thank you.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.862
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I've
really been looking forward

00:07:03.862 --> 00:07:06.240
to this conversation because
I'm familiar with your work,

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.390
and I know that many
people are going

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.820
to be excited to
learn about your work

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.800
as it relates to
sleep, as it relates

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.750
to problem solving, creativity,
addiction and craving, relapse,

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:19.800
and a number of other
important topics.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.310
So to start things
off, I would love

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.680
for you to educate us
a bit about this thing

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.380
that we are all familiar
with and yet very few of us

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.700
understand, which is sleep.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.620
And if you would, could you
describe the various phases

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:34.820
of sleep that exist?

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.230
What distinguish them?

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:39.080
And perhaps frame this
within the context

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.750
of what would a perfect
night's sleep look like?

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:42.380
GINA POE: OK.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: How long
would it last, more or less,

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:47.450
and what would the
biology look like?

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:49.683
What is a perfect night's sleep?

00:07:49.683 --> 00:07:51.350
GINA POE: Yeah, that's
a great question.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:51.850
All right.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.350
So sleep is really
different from wakefulness,

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:58.250
and in fact can't be replaced
by any state of wakefulness

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:01.100
that we've been able
to come up with so far.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.630
Our brain chemistry is
completely different.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:05.430
And in the different
stages of sleep,

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:08.660
which there is non-REM and
REM are the two major states

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.060
of sleep, and every animal
we've studied so far

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.520
seems to have both
of those states.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.370
Anyway, those two states are
entirely different from one

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.000
another, too.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.760
And even within non-REM,
there are three states.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:22.700
Stage one, which is what
you slip into when you first

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:24.818
falling asleep-- it's dozing.

00:08:24.818 --> 00:08:26.360
There's kind of an
interesting rhythm

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:27.443
that goes on in the brain.

00:08:27.443 --> 00:08:29.300
It's kind of a
fast, gamma rhythm.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.670
And then there's
stage two, which

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:35.000
is a really cool state we
sort of used to ignore,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.159
sleep researchers, because
it was a transient state

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:39.620
between wakefulness
and the deep stage

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.020
three, slow wave sleep, which
is the most impressively

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.760
different.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:46.200
And between that and REM sleep.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.720
So stage two, I'll talk
a little bit more about.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.600
And then the deep,
slow wave sleep state,

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.400
which is when big, slow waves
sweep through our brain.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.232
And now we realize that
it cleans our brain.

00:08:56.232 --> 00:08:58.190
One of the things that
those big, slow waves do

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:01.070
is cleans our brain and does
other really important things

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.060
to restore us from a
day of wakefulness.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.250
And then REM sleep,
which is the most popular

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:09.740
because that's where we
have the most active dreams.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:13.790
And when you wake up
someone out of REM sleep,

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.010
they'll almost always
report having dreamed

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:17.420
something really bizarre.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:20.010
That's called REM sleep,
rapid eye movement sleep.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.940
So those are the four states
of sleep, of human sleep,

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:26.630
and we cycle through them
every 90 minutes or so.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:30.440
When we go to sleep,
say 10:30, 11:00,

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:34.580
our first REM sleep period
comes about 105 minutes

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:38.450
after we fall asleep and
lasts about 20 minutes.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:41.780
Actually, it comes
about 95 minutes

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.070
and lasts 10 or 15 minutes.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:47.150
And then we start over again.

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:49.790
And we have about five of those
per night for a perfect night's

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:50.300
sleep--

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.980
four or five,
something like that.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:55.670
So a perfect night's sleep is
seven and a half, eight hours.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:58.070
There was a really
great study that

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:01.130
put people in a semi-darkened
room with nothing

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:04.430
but the bed for 12 hours
every day for a month.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:06.740
And what people did
initially, because we're

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:09.100
in a sleep deprived
nation, is that they

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:13.360
slept a lot more than usual,
like 10 or 11 hours of the 12.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:16.630
And then they leveled
off after a week or two

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:19.000
to about eight hours
and 15 minutes of sleep.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.980
So you actually can't oversleep.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:23.290
I mean, they had nothing
else to do but sleep,

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:26.380
and they would round off to
an average eight hours and 15

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.960
minutes a night and then spend
the rest of the time twiddling

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:30.242
their thumbs, humming tunes--

00:10:30.242 --> 00:10:31.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:32.240
GINA POE: --daydreaming.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:32.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I want to get back

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:34.550
to the contour of a
perfect night's sleep,

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:37.600
but I'm intrigued by this idea
that people can't oversleep.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.840
I'm often asked whether or
not we can get too much sleep

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.290
and whether or not sleeping
too long can make us groggy

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:45.580
the next day.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:47.780
Is there anything to that?

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.530
And how does one
determine how long

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:52.375
they should sleep on average?

00:10:52.375 --> 00:10:53.500
GINA POE: On average, yeah.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.040
Well, that's interesting
because different people seem

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.990
to need different
amounts of sleep,

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:02.260
but we don't really even know
exactly what sleep is for.

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:06.490
So what they need is kind of--

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:10.300
it's murky.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:14.720
So we do know a lot of things
that sleep does now for us,

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:17.020
but we don't know how
long those things take.

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:21.500
So how long we need to sleep is
also just a big question mark.

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:23.080
But some people
don't feel rested

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:24.860
until they've slept nine hours.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:28.810
Some people don't feel rested
after three or four and a half.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:32.897
But most people, if they
consistently deprive themselves

00:11:32.897 --> 00:11:34.480
of sleep so that
they're only sleeping

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:36.310
for four and a
half hours a night,

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:41.360
build up a cognitive deficit
that just builds up over time.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.160
The more nights you have with
sleep deprivation, the more

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.850
cognitive deficit you have.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:50.980
And so you need sleep, again,
to sleep more to recover.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:53.770
Now, the question
you had about um--

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Can you oversleep--

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:56.170
GINA POE: Can you
oversleep, right.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:56.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Can
you sleep to the point

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:58.210
where it's too much?

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:02.410
You know, growing up,
when I was in high school,

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:04.330
my girlfriend's
dad had this belief

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.270
that no one should
sleep in past 6:00 AM.

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:09.010
So he would wake all the--

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:10.510
there were two
children in the home.

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:12.335
He would wake up the
kids in that house.

00:12:12.335 --> 00:12:13.960
He had this thing
against oversleeping,

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.150
regardless of when
people went to sleep,

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:18.790
and I always thought that
was an interesting mentality.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:19.600
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:22.330
It's not terrible, actually,
because what that will do

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:25.540
is it will make you
sleepier the next night

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:26.740
to get to bed on time.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.470
So it'll build up
your homeostatic need

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:30.670
if you wake up too early.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:34.180
But so I don't think
you can oversleep.

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:36.130
But people who sleep
a lot, like people

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.500
who sleep over nine
hours, it's probably

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:41.960
indicative of some other
problem because in fact,

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.957
if you have a lot of
different conditions,

00:12:44.957 --> 00:12:46.540
it will cause you
to sleep a lot more.

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:47.998
Probably because
what it does is it

00:12:47.998 --> 00:12:49.840
interferes with your
efficient sleep,

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:51.350
the efficiency of your sleep.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:55.300
So if you find yourself
sleeping consistently nine hours

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.640
plus every night,
then you might want

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.790
to consult a doctor about
maybe what else it might be.

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:02.410
It could be cancer.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:07.160
It could be sleep apnea,
which affects a lot of people.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:09.160
It could be that your
sleep is super inefficient

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.380
because you're snoring a
lot more than you know,

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:14.350
or you're waking up a lot more
than you know every night.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:16.750
So you might want
a sleep study just

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:19.540
to see how your
sleep is, and then

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:22.300
see what else might be
causing you to sleep so much.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:23.717
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And that wouldn't

00:13:23.717 --> 00:13:25.480
be if somebody is
sleeping nine or 10

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.340
hours every once in a while.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.050
You mean if they're
consistently sleeping

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:30.520
for more than nine hours.

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:32.562
GINA POE: If they feel
like they need it in order

00:13:32.562 --> 00:13:34.760
to function cognitively
the next day,

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:37.360
it might be that your sleep
is just not efficient,

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:40.592
and you might want to look
into why that's the case.

00:13:40.592 --> 00:13:41.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.800
Forgive me for the anecdote,
but I can't resist.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.820
Years ago I went to
an acupuncturist,

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:50.288
and he gave me these
red pills, of which I

00:13:50.288 --> 00:13:51.580
don't know what they contained.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:52.180
GINA POE: Uh-oh.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:53.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But I took
them because he told me

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.090
they would help with my sleep.

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:55.420
GINA POE: OK.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:57.087
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
I would fall asleep

00:13:57.087 --> 00:13:58.750
about 30 minutes
after taking them,

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:02.920
and I would have
incredibly vivid dreams.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:05.380
And I'd wake up
four or five hours

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:08.350
after having gone to sleep
feeling completely rested,

00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:10.840
something that I've
never really experienced

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.070
on a consistent basis.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:13.970
I want to do mass
spec on these pills.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:16.120
I still have no idea what
was in them, whatsoever.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:17.380
GINA POE: I want to do mass
spec on those pills, too.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:18.672
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, exactly.

00:14:18.672 --> 00:14:21.857
Some people thought perhaps they
had GHB, gamma hydroxybutyrate,

00:14:21.857 --> 00:14:23.440
which is, by the
way, an illegal drug.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:24.160
It can kill you.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:25.720
It's not something
you want to take.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:26.560
GINA POE: No, that's not good.

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:29.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But anyway,
if ever someone can figure out

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:30.800
what the red pills
were, I'll be very--

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:32.050
GINA POE: That's really great.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And this is
not a red pill of the other sort

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:34.590
red pill.

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:36.280
This is just the
red sleep pills.

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:37.197
GINA POE: Interesting.

00:14:37.197 --> 00:14:39.400
I mean, it could have
been even a placebo effect

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.663
because placebo is
extremely strong.

00:14:41.663 --> 00:14:43.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Although, I don't know.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:45.247
There was really something
to these red pills.

00:14:45.247 --> 00:14:47.830
So shout out to
the acupuncturists

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:50.560
in Eastern medicine.

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:54.640
But to return this to this
idea of the architecture

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:55.990
of a perfect night's sleep.

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:58.660
So you said we fall asleep.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:01.440
The first 90 minutes of sleep.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.360
REM sleep, rapid
eye movement sleep,

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.580
will arrive at
about 95 minutes in.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:09.090
Does that mean that the
rest of that 90 minutes

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:11.460
is consumed with
slow wave sleep?

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:12.810
GINA POE: Yeah, non-REM sleep.

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:13.643
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:15:13.643 --> 00:15:16.740
And what about the sleep
where we are lightly asleep,

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:20.010
and we might have a
dream that has us somehow

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.500
thinking about movement or
that we jolt ourselves awake?

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:24.375
That often happens early
in the night, right?

00:15:24.375 --> 00:15:26.340
GINA POE: Yeah, that's
the first stage--

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:28.380
stage one and
stage two of sleep.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:30.780
And stage two sleep
is really cool

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:34.710
because that has something
called sleep spindles

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:35.880
and K-complexes.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:40.710
And what sleep spindles are are
a little bit of activity that's

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:43.990
10 to 15 hertz in frequency.

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:47.200
It's a conversation between
the thalamus and the cortex.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.770
The thalamus is the
gateway to consciousness,

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:53.100
and the neocortex processes
all our cognition.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:57.720
And so it's these spindles.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:59.490
They're called sleep spindles.

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.530
And if you wake up
out of that state,

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:05.040
you will often report a dream,
like a hallucination style

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:05.580
dream.

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:09.180
It won't be a long dream report
like you have out of REM sleep,

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:11.790
but it will be some
hallucination state.

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:13.830
And while we're
falling asleep, one

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:15.540
of the reasons we
call it falling asleep

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:17.430
is because in stage
one and stage two

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:19.140
our muscles are relaxing.

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:22.560
And if there's part of our
brain that's conscious enough

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:24.900
to sort of recognize
that relaxation,

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:28.020
we'll feel like we're
falling, and we'll jerk awake.

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.440
So often that hallucination--
it's called hypnagogic

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:33.510
hallucination-- will feel like--

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:35.970
it'll include some
falling aspect

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:37.528
that we'll wake up out of.

00:16:37.528 --> 00:16:39.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's
really interesting to me.

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:43.500
I've long felt that
sensation of almost, like,

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:45.990
dropping back into my head.

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:48.810
So much so that if I elevate
my feet just slightly

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.330
and I tilt my head back
just slightly in order

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:53.715
to go to sleep, I find that
I fall asleep much faster.

00:16:53.715 --> 00:16:54.270
GINA POE: Interesting.

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:54.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
But it does feel

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:56.160
as if I'm going to
fall, like, almost going

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.410
to do a backward somersault.
I actually really like

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:00.598
the sensation and
usually because it

00:17:00.598 --> 00:17:01.890
precedes falling deeply asleep.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:03.780
GINA POE: Yeah, that's
really interesting.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:06.990
Somebody has to do a study
of elevated feet and--

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:10.378
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, there's
a little bit on body position

00:17:10.378 --> 00:17:12.670
and sleep in some of the
washout that we'll talk about.

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:16.890
So early in the night you've got
these lighter stages of sleep,

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.900
less rapid eye movement sleep.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.750
What can we say about the
dreams that occur during the,

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:26.730
say, first and second
90-minute cycles of sleep.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.040
Are they quite different
than the patterns

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:31.530
of sleep and dreaming that
occur later in the night

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:32.430
or toward morning?

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:34.670
GINA POE: Well, OK, that's
an interesting question,

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:37.030
and there's a lot
of facets to it.

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:41.220
There is some evidence that
the first four hours of sleep

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:44.280
are very important
for memory processing.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:49.960
And in fact, if you've
learned something new that day

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:54.730
or have experienced a new
sensory motor experience,

00:17:54.730 --> 00:17:58.360
then your early
sleep dreams will

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.060
incorporate that experience
much more than the later sleep

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:02.200
dreams.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.330
Later, as that memory
gets consolidated

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:07.300
from the early
structures, which are

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:10.330
the hippocampus deep in the
temporal lobe to the cortex

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:13.570
in a distributed
fashion, that memory

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:17.200
seems to move from that
hippocampus to the cortex.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:20.530
And also the dreams that
incorporate that memory also

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:21.670
move later in the night.

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:25.870
So nobody knows
why, but it does.

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:29.230
There was a great study
by Siddhartha Ribeiro

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:33.940
who studied the consolidation
of memories from the hippocampus

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:39.310
to the cortex in a rat across
the period of a full day's

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.560
sleep because rats
sleep in the daytime.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:45.310
And he found that each
subsequent REM sleep period

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:48.010
moved that memory
from the hippocampus

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.000
to the first area
that projects to it,

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.410
and then the second area,
and then the third area.

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:57.930
And you can see the memory
moving throughout the sleep

00:18:57.930 --> 00:18:58.430
period.

00:18:58.430 --> 00:18:58.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Cool.

00:18:58.780 --> 00:18:59.640
Very cool.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:01.340
I have to read that study.

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:05.320
So there's a number of
different hormones associated

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:06.970
with the different
stages of sleep.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:09.132
We know that melatonin
is a hormone--

00:19:09.132 --> 00:19:10.090
GINA POE: Of nighttime.

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:12.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --of
nighttime that makes us sleepy.

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:14.260
What about growth
hormone release?

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:15.860
When does that
occur during sleep?

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:17.318
GINA POE: So growth
hormone release

00:19:17.318 --> 00:19:19.120
happens all day long
and all night long,

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:21.580
but the deep, slow
wave sleep that you

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:25.300
get the very first
sleep cycle is

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:28.540
when you get a big bolus
of growth hormone release,

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:31.040
in men and women equally.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:34.870
And if you miss that first
deep, slow wave sleep period,

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:38.500
you also miss that big bolus
of growth hormone release.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:42.370
And you might get, ultimately
across the day, just as much

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:45.310
overall growth hormone
release, but endocrinologists

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:48.370
will tell you that big
boluses do different things

00:19:48.370 --> 00:19:50.630
than a little bit
eked out over time.

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:53.260
So that is what we know.

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:58.970
There's also a big push
to synthesize proteins.

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:02.050
So that's when the
protein synthesis

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:05.080
part that builds memories,
for example, in our brain

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:08.170
happens in that
first cycle of sleep.

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:10.480
So you don't want to miss
that, especially if you've

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:14.140
learned something really big
and needs more synaptic space

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:14.752
to encode it.

00:20:14.752 --> 00:20:16.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
How would somebody

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:17.548
miss that first 90 minutes?

00:20:17.548 --> 00:20:19.090
GINA POE: Sleep
depriving themselves.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:19.990
Yeah.

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:21.657
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
let's say I normally

00:20:21.657 --> 00:20:24.160
go to sleep at 10:00 PM.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:26.410
And then from 10:00
to 11:30 would

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.180
be this first phase
of sleep, and that's

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:31.520
when the big bolus of growth
hormone would be released.

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:33.280
Does that mean that
if I go to sleep

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:35.860
instead at 11:30 or
midnight that I missed

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:37.180
that first phase of sleep?

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:40.330
Why is it not the case that I
get that first phase of sleep

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:41.890
just simply starting later?

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:44.110
GINA POE: It is
a beautiful clock

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:45.790
that we have in
our body that knows

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:46.990
when things should happen.

00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:49.510
And it's-- every cell
in our body has a clock.

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:52.690
And all of those clocks
are normally synchronized.

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:56.020
And those circadian
clocks are synchronized.

00:20:56.020 --> 00:21:01.630
And so our cells are ready to
respond to that growth hormone

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:03.410
release at a particular time.

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:06.820
And if we miss it-- and it's a
time in relation to melatonin

00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:07.480
also--

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:10.940
so if you miss it, yeah, you
might get some growth hormone

00:21:10.940 --> 00:21:11.440
release.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:14.500
But it's occurring at a time
when your clock has already

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:15.910
moved to the next phase.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.143
And so it's just a clock thing.

00:21:18.143 --> 00:21:19.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:22.090
I don't think we can overstate
the importance of what you just

00:21:22.090 --> 00:21:22.630
described.

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:24.760
And to be honest,
despite knowing a bit

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:26.920
about the sleep research
in circadian biology,

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:30.580
this is the very first time
that I've ever heard this

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:34.208
that if you normally go to sleep
at a particular time and growth

00:21:34.208 --> 00:21:36.250
hormone is released in
that first phase of sleep,

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:38.620
that you can't simply
initiate your sleep out

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:41.680
later and expect to capture
that first phase of sleep.

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:42.430
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:43.847
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's incredible

00:21:43.847 --> 00:21:46.780
and I think
important and as many

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:49.240
listeners are probably realizing
also highly actionable.

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:50.740
So what this means
is that we should

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:53.110
have fairly consistent
bedtimes in addition

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:54.747
to fairly consistent wake times.

00:21:54.747 --> 00:21:55.330
Is that right?

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:56.080
GINA POE: Exactly.

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:58.870
And in fact, one
of the best markers

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:01.720
of good neurological
health when we get older

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:04.415
is consistent bedtimes.

00:22:04.415 --> 00:22:05.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Wow.

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:07.030
OK.

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:08.410
I don't want to backtrack.

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:10.420
But I did write down
something that I

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:14.500
think is important for me to
resolve or for you to resolve.

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:16.390
So I'm going to ask
this people that

00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:20.620
sleep nine hours or more perhaps
that reflecting an issue--

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:23.800
some underlying
issue, perhaps, is

00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:25.660
being a teenager
or an adolescent

00:22:25.660 --> 00:22:28.420
and undergoing a
stage of development

00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:30.370
where there's a lot of
bodily and brain growth

00:22:30.370 --> 00:22:31.570
and exception to that, because--

00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:32.110
GINA POE: Yes.

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:34.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --I don't
recall sleeping a ton

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:35.240
when I was a teenager.

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:36.610
I had a ton of energy.

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:39.070
But I know a few teenagers.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:40.677
And they sleep a lot.

00:22:40.677 --> 00:22:41.260
GINA POE: Yeah

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:42.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Like they'll
just sleep, and sleep,

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:43.090
and sleep, and sleep.

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:44.800
Should we let them sleep,
and sleep, and sleep?

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:45.670
GINA POE: Let them sleep.

00:22:45.670 --> 00:22:45.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:47.050
So that's the one exception.

00:22:47.050 --> 00:22:47.410
What about--

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:48.280
GINA POE: Just like babies.

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:49.113
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:22:49.113 --> 00:22:51.610
GINA POE: When you're developing
something in your brain

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:53.530
or the rest of your
body, you really

00:22:53.530 --> 00:22:56.320
need sleep to help
organize that.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:59.710
Sleep is doing really hard
work in organizing our brains

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:01.390
and making it develop right.

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:03.760
And if we deprive
ourselves of sleep,

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:06.560
we will-- actually,
also just like I said,

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:08.260
we have a daily clock.

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:10.000
We also have a
developmental clock.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.610
And we can miss a
developmental window

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:16.455
if we don't let ourselves
sleep extra like we need to.

00:23:16.455 --> 00:23:18.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What other
things inhibit growth hormone

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:21.410
release or other components
of this first stage of sleep?

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:23.720
In other words, if I
go to sleep religiously

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:26.090
every night at
10:00 PM, are there

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:28.760
things that I, perhaps,
do in the preceding

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:32.510
hours of the preceding day
like ingest caffeine or alcohol

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:34.370
that can make that
first stage of sleep

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:37.070
less effective even if I'm
going to sleep at the same time?

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:39.800
GINA POE: Alcohol definitely
will do that because alcohol

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:41.180
is a REM sleep suppressant.

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:43.370
And it even suppresses
some of that stage two

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:45.392
transition to REM with
those sleep spindles.

00:23:45.392 --> 00:23:46.850
And those sleep
spindles, we didn't

00:23:46.850 --> 00:23:48.600
talk about their
function yet, but they're

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:52.640
really important for moving
memories to our cortex.

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:55.750
It's a unique time
when our hippocampus,

00:23:55.750 --> 00:24:01.490
like the RAM of our brains,
writes it to a hard disk which

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:02.360
is the cortex.

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:04.920
And it's a unique time
when they're connected.

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:06.840
So if you don't
want to miss that,

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:08.960
you don't want to miss
REM sleep when it's also

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:13.160
a part of the consolidation
process and schema changing

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:17.538
process, and alcohol in
there before we go to sleep

00:24:17.538 --> 00:24:18.080
will do that.

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:21.950
Until we've metabolized alcohol
and put it out of our bodies,

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:24.650
it will affect our sleep badly.

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:26.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
probably fair to say

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:30.720
no ingestion of alcohol
within the four to six

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:31.850
hours preceding sleep?

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:32.150
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:24:32.150 --> 00:24:33.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Given the half life?

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:33.710
GINA POE: Given the half--

00:24:33.710 --> 00:24:35.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Or at all--
or at all would be better.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.520
But I know some people
refuse to go that way.

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.560
GINA POE: And maybe
a little bit is OK.

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:41.720
I don't know what
the dose response is.

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:44.392
But there are studies out
there you can look at.

00:24:44.392 --> 00:24:45.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:46.310
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:48.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So we're still
in the first stage of sleep.

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:49.790
And I apologize for
slowing us down.

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:52.940
But it sounds like it's
an incredibly important,

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:54.470
first phase of sleep.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:57.590
What about the second and third
90-minute blocks of sleep?

00:24:57.590 --> 00:25:00.750
Is there anything that
makes those unique?

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:03.710
What is their signature
besides the fact that they come

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:05.000
second and third in the night?

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:06.800
GINA POE: There's more
and more REM sleep

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:08.900
the later the night we get.

00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:11.600
There's also a
change in hormones.

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:16.700
The growth hormone and melatonin
levels are starting to decline.

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:18.930
But other hormones
are picking up.

00:25:18.930 --> 00:25:21.740
So it is a really
different stage

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:24.740
that you also don't want
to shortchange yourself on.

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:27.650
And I think that's the stage
many studies are showing

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:30.020
that those are the
times in sleep when

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:32.210
the most creativity can happen.

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:35.450
That's when our dreams can
incorporate and put together

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:40.040
old and new things
together into a new way.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:42.630
And our schema are
built during that time.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:46.340
So yeah, we can
change our minds best

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:47.600
during those phases of sleep.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:49.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could you
elaborate a little bit more

00:25:49.850 --> 00:25:50.515
on schema?

00:25:50.515 --> 00:25:51.140
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:51.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No one--

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.000
I don't think anyone
on this podcast

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:54.470
has ever discussed schema.

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:56.360
I'm a little bit
familiar with schema

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:58.473
from my courses on psychology.

00:25:58.473 --> 00:25:59.390
But it's been a while.

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:01.640
So maybe you could just
refresh mind and everyone--

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:03.213
GINA POE: Well, it's
still a concept.

00:26:03.213 --> 00:26:04.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sure.

00:26:04.130 --> 00:26:04.610
GINA POE: Schema--

00:26:04.610 --> 00:26:06.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN: How do you--
how do you define schema?

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:07.070
GINA POE: Right.

00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:11.030
I think of schema as--

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:14.030
like we have a schema
of Christmas, right?

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:17.630
We have all kinds of ideas
that were so together

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:20.330
and called Christmas,
a holiday season.

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:22.550
In the northern
hemisphere, it's cold.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:26.720
We have Santa Claus, and
reindeer, and jingle bells,

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:30.770
and even things that are
false but we normally

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:33.860
associate with Christmas
presents, family gathering when

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:34.430
it is.

00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:37.160
All of this stuff is
sewn together into one--

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:39.020
there's a thread
linking them all.

00:26:39.020 --> 00:26:44.330
And we can just give ourselves a
list of words, and none of them

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:45.800
contain the word Christmas.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:50.240
And then ask people later,
give them another list of words

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:51.590
and include the word Christmas.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:53.780
And they'll say, oh,
yeah, that word was there,

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:55.490
because in their
minds they brought up

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:58.890
that word, Christmas, because
it's part of that whole schema.

00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:02.360
So that's what-- it's
sort of a related--

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:04.430
lot of related
concepts, I guess.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:07.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think about
the desktop of my computer

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:08.910
would scare some people.

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:10.400
But it's just a ton of folders.

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:10.640
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:11.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
each of the folder names

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:13.753
mean something very
clear and specific to me.

00:27:13.753 --> 00:27:14.420
GINA POE: Right.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:15.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And inside of those

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:17.970
folders are collections
of things that make sense

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:19.220
in terms of how they're batch.

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:20.540
Is that one way to think about--

00:27:20.540 --> 00:27:22.540
GINA POE: No, that's a
great way to think of it.

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:24.920
And when you're in REM
sleep in the later parts

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:27.200
of the night and that
transition to REM,

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:30.050
that's when your
computer of your brain

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:33.530
is opening folders and comparing
documents, seeing if-- is there

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:35.120
anything the same?

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:37.130
These two documents
look very much the same,

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:39.620
but there's a little
bit of difference.

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:41.840
And it can link
those conceptually.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:44.720
So that's probably one of
the origins of creativity

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:47.300
is finding things
that are related,

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:49.340
maybe just linked a little bit.

00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:51.680
And you can find that
link and strengthen it

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:56.030
if it makes your schema
interesting and different.

00:27:56.030 --> 00:27:58.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
very interesting.

00:27:58.010 --> 00:28:00.650
Many people, including
myself tend to wake

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:02.930
up maybe once during
the middle of the night

00:28:02.930 --> 00:28:03.830
to use the restroom.

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:06.665
I've tried to drink less
fluid before going to sleep.

00:28:06.665 --> 00:28:12.538
I've heard also that the impulse
to urinate, forgive the topic,

00:28:12.538 --> 00:28:15.080
but a lot of people deal with
this, so the impulse to urinate

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:17.850
is also dictated by how quickly
you drink fluid, not just

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:18.670
the total volume.

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:20.310
So I've switched
to sipping fluids

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:22.770
more slowly from my last
beverage of the day which

00:28:22.770 --> 00:28:23.537
seems to help.

00:28:23.537 --> 00:28:25.620
But the point here is that
I think a lot of people

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:27.703
wake up once in the middle
of the night oftentimes

00:28:27.703 --> 00:28:30.600
to use the restroom but
oftentimes just around 3:00 AM

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:32.487
and might be up
for a few minutes,

00:28:32.487 --> 00:28:35.070
hopefully not on their phone or
viewing any bright light which

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:38.310
can cause more wakefulness,
but then go back to sleep.

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:42.810
Is there any known detriment to
this middle of the night waking

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:44.310
or should we
consider it a normal

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:46.575
feature for some people's
sleep architecture?

00:28:46.575 --> 00:28:47.200
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:49.350
I think we shouldn't
worry about it actually.

00:28:49.350 --> 00:28:53.340
I think sleep is really
incredibly well homeostatically

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:53.970
regulated.

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:57.210
And so really, don't worry
about how much you're sleeping,

00:28:57.210 --> 00:28:58.920
as long as you're
not intentionally

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:01.260
depriving yourself of sleep
by doing something really

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:03.630
rewarding and exciting
because even that is

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:06.270
stressful to your body and
deprives you of a lot of things

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:07.540
we're talking about.

00:29:07.540 --> 00:29:10.330
So don't worry about it.

00:29:10.330 --> 00:29:12.990
It's absolutely normal
to wake up at least

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:15.390
once in the middle of the
night to go to the bathroom.

00:29:15.390 --> 00:29:17.310
And as long as you
can get back to sleep

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:20.370
in a reasonable
amount of time or even

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:22.680
if it takes you an hour,
don't worry about it,

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:26.430
as long as you have a lifestyle
that allows you to then make up

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:29.910
that sleep either the next
morning or the next night

00:29:29.910 --> 00:29:31.660
or going to bed
a little earlier.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:32.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So if
I understand correctly,

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:34.860
there's a little bit of
asymmetry to sleep that

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:38.040
catching that first phase of
sleep it's like you either get

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:38.890
it or you don't.

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:40.560
And you have to get
it by going to sleep

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:43.710
essentially the same time each
night, maybe plus or minus

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:44.675
15 minutes or so.

00:29:44.675 --> 00:29:45.300
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:47.467
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But then
if I wake up in the middle

00:29:47.467 --> 00:29:49.770
of the night and go back
to sleep I cannot catch up,

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:52.418
but I can gather all the sleep
that I would have gotten had I

00:29:52.418 --> 00:29:54.210
just slept the whole
way through the night.

00:29:54.210 --> 00:29:54.720
Is that right?

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:55.845
GINA POE: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:29:55.845 --> 00:29:58.110
And we don't know
actually the answer to

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:01.140
whether or not the sleep in the
middle between that early sleep

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:04.980
and the late sleep is, in fact,
different for another reason.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:07.620
And whether depriving
yourself of sleep from say,

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:11.380
1:00 to 2:30 in the morning
is bad in a different way,

00:30:11.380 --> 00:30:12.270
we don't know.

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:13.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, I suppose I

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:15.060
am the experiment in
that case, because I do

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:16.352
tend to wake up once per night.

00:30:16.352 --> 00:30:20.338
And I've come to recognize
it as part of my normal sleep

00:30:20.338 --> 00:30:20.880
architecture.

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:22.080
I don't obsess over it.

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:24.060
I do notice that when
I go back to sleep

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:27.820
and especially toward morning
that my sleep is incredibly

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:28.320
deep.

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:29.822
My dreams are incredibly vivid.

00:30:29.822 --> 00:30:30.780
GINA POE: That's great.

00:30:30.780 --> 00:30:32.697
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I don't
always remember them.

00:30:32.697 --> 00:30:35.910
But what is unique, perhaps,
about the architecture

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:37.860
of dreams and
sleep in the, let's

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.708
say the last third of the
night or the second half

00:30:40.708 --> 00:30:41.250
of the night?

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:41.660
GINA POE: Right, yeah.

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:43.118
In the second half
of the night, we

00:30:43.118 --> 00:30:44.970
have longer REM sleep periods.

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:48.300
And those are considered
the deepest sleep,

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:52.380
even though slow wave sleep, big
slow wave is considered deep.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:53.220
It is deep.

00:30:53.220 --> 00:30:55.428
ANDREW HUBERMAN: They call
slow wave sleep deep sleep

00:30:55.428 --> 00:30:56.261
and REM sleep rapid.

00:30:56.261 --> 00:30:58.470
But now, you're telling me
that REM sleep is actually

00:30:58.470 --> 00:30:59.220
the deeper sleep?

00:30:59.220 --> 00:30:59.730
OK.

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:01.200
There needs to be
a new nomenclature

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:01.965
of sleep researchers.

00:31:01.965 --> 00:31:03.780
GINA POE: You really shouldn't
call it deeper night.

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:04.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No, please.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:05.380
GINA POE: The
reason why they call

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:07.410
slow wave sleep deep
sleep is because it's

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:10.920
difficult to arouse
people out of that state.

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:12.930
And when you do arouse
them out of that state,

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:16.650
they are most often confused and
just want to go back into sleep

00:31:16.650 --> 00:31:18.990
and can go back pretty easily.

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:20.740
If you arouse someone
out of REM sleep,

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:22.740
they're more likely to
report something that was

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:24.710
really almost like wakefulness.

00:31:24.710 --> 00:31:26.430
It was so vivid.

00:31:26.430 --> 00:31:32.580
But in fact, if you give
someone a non-threatening kind

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:37.470
of stimulation like somebody
dropping keys, or a ping,

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:39.480
or something like that,
instead of waking--

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:44.070
that same volume will wake
someone up out of non-REM sleep

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:47.610
but out of REM sleep and instead
lengthen the amount of time

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:49.680
or make it even more dense--

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:51.270
your rapid eye
movements more dense.

00:31:51.270 --> 00:31:53.640
And often people will
incorporate that sound

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:55.007
into their dreams.

00:31:55.007 --> 00:31:57.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So the
body and brain are somehow

00:31:57.090 --> 00:32:00.360
conscious of the of the sound.

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:02.460
And I've heard also
smells can even make it

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:05.080
into our dreams in REM sleep.

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:07.338
But that it doesn't rouse us--

00:32:07.338 --> 00:32:09.255
GINA POE: It doesn't
arouse us as often, yeah.

00:32:09.255 --> 00:32:09.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:12.420
GINA POE: And maybe one of
the reasons why REM sleep

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:16.020
is deeper especially in adults
and older people, that deep

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:17.560
slow wave sleep goes away.

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:19.290
So it's not as deep.

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:20.370
It's not as big.

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:23.670
The slow waves aren't as large
which is probably problematic,

00:32:23.670 --> 00:32:24.900
but we are not sure.

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:28.680
And so then REM sleep
becomes the deepest stage.

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:31.860
Actually in children,
it's kind of a toss

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:34.200
up, because it's really
hard to wake them up out

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:35.460
of that deep slow wave sleep.

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:39.870
And in fact, fire alarms
don't wake them up,

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:43.480
even really loud fire alarms
out of that state of sleep.

00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:46.860
So that's why they're trying
to change fire alarms so

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:51.270
that instead of something
that the kids don't associate

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:54.720
with anything like the,
whatever, they don't associate

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:57.270
them, it says their
name or something else

00:32:57.270 --> 00:33:01.920
that may be less loud
but more salient to them

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:02.880
and will wake them up.

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:04.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I don't
know having carried

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:05.573
sleeping children
in from the car,

00:33:05.573 --> 00:33:07.740
I don't know that I want
children to start waking up

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:09.600
from sleep because that's
one of the best things when

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:11.790
we get home and the kids
are asleep in the backseat.

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:13.890
You can literally throw
them over your shoulder,

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:16.450
gently of course, and
put them to sleep.

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:18.028
They are completely out.

00:33:18.028 --> 00:33:19.320
GINA POE: Yeah, it's wonderful.

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:20.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It is wonderful,

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:22.380
one of nature's gifts.

