00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:41.460
So this enhanced
volume or proportion

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:44.460
of rapid eye movement sleep in
the second half of the night

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:47.130
relates to more
elaborate dreams.

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:49.560
We are paralyzed during
REM sleep, correct?

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.239
GINA POE: Yes,
normally paralyzed.

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:53.880
And that's really
good, because that's

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:57.030
the time when we're actively
dreaming storyline dreams.

00:34:57.030 --> 00:34:59.400
And we could hurt ourselves.

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:03.000
We're actually really cut
off from the outside world

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:07.590
in terms of responding
to, say, this table,

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:09.670
or window, or a door.

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:12.780
And so different from
sleepwalking which

00:35:12.780 --> 00:35:13.980
is out of slow wave sleep.

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:15.855
And out of slow wave
sleep, that sleepwalking

00:35:15.855 --> 00:35:18.052
is a mixture between
sleep and wakefulness.

00:35:18.052 --> 00:35:19.760
So you actually will
respond to the door,

00:35:19.760 --> 00:35:23.750
or you can cook a full meal,
drive your car while you're

00:35:23.750 --> 00:35:24.750
in deep slow wave sleep.

00:35:24.750 --> 00:35:27.490
It's scary, because you never
know what you're going to do.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:30.240
You don't have voluntary,
involuntary control over it.

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:31.900
You have no conscious
control over it.

00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:34.800
But you can actually
safely navigate

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:39.060
some situations in
sleepwalking and actually have

00:35:39.060 --> 00:35:40.050
a conversation.

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:42.300
Although, it may not make
much sense when you're sleep

00:35:42.300 --> 00:35:43.110
talking.

00:35:43.110 --> 00:35:47.190
In REM sleep, you're not
processing the outside world.

00:35:47.190 --> 00:35:50.340
And instead, when you're
acting out your dreams,

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:53.310
you could be doing things like
walking through a plate glass

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:58.360
window or falling off of down
the stairs, things like that.

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:01.680
So you really want
your muscles to be

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:03.660
inactivated during REM sleep.

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:05.700
Otherwise, you will
act out those dreams

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:08.940
and really hurt yourself
or your bed partner.

00:36:08.940 --> 00:36:11.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What about
sleep talking or talking

00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:12.660
in sleep?

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:14.940
I don't know how many
relationships have

00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:16.320
been saved by sleep talking.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:18.970
But I'm guessing a few
have been destroyed.

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:22.740
And I'm guessing that talking
in sleep could have meaning

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:25.050
or perhaps has no
meaning just as dreams

00:36:25.050 --> 00:36:27.485
could have meaning or no
meaning, as we recall them.

00:36:27.485 --> 00:36:28.110
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:36:28.110 --> 00:36:30.870
Do not take sleep talking
seriously no matter

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:31.950
what people say.

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:34.080
It doesn't necessarily
reflect truth.

00:36:34.080 --> 00:36:36.510
So it's not like you're
being more truthful when

00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:37.500
you're sleep talking.

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:39.917
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You just saved
a number of relationships.

00:36:39.917 --> 00:36:41.100
GINA POE: I hope so.

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:42.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm not
directing this at anyone

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:42.930
in particular.

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:45.930
But I guarantee you just did.

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:47.700
Noted.

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:51.510
So as people start to
approach morning or the time

00:36:51.510 --> 00:36:54.250
when they normally
would wake up,

00:36:54.250 --> 00:36:57.870
I've heard that it's
important to, if possible,

00:36:57.870 --> 00:37:01.260
complete one of these 90-minute
cycles prior to waking up.

00:37:01.260 --> 00:37:04.020
That is if you set
your alarm for halfway

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:06.450
through one of these 90-minute
cycles that come late

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:11.100
in the night of sleep,
that it can lead to rather

00:37:11.100 --> 00:37:13.920
groggy patterns of waking.

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:15.840
So I'll just ask you directly.

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:17.610
Do you use an alarm clock?

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:18.780
GINA POE: I do not.

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:20.610
Thankfully, I'm
in a line of work

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:23.460
that doesn't require me
normally to do anything

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:24.600
at any particular time.

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:25.855
I do it when I do it.

00:37:25.855 --> 00:37:27.480
Unless I have to
catch a plane and then

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:29.290
I always set my
alarm just in case.

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:30.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
as a fellow academic,

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:32.958
I can tell you there are
plenty of punishing features

00:37:32.958 --> 00:37:35.430
about being an
academic scientist that

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:37.900
offset the fact that you don't
have to use an alarm clock.

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:40.620
But it is nice that you can
often set your own schedule.

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:42.660
So would you recommend
that if possible

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:44.250
that people not
use an alarm clock?

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:45.720
GINA POE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:37:45.720 --> 00:37:47.310
If you can just
listen to your body

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:51.130
and wake up when you need to
wake up, that would be great.

00:37:51.130 --> 00:37:55.200
But one of the reasons why we
have such a grogginess, it's

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:56.820
called sleep inertia
when we wake up

00:37:56.820 --> 00:38:00.510
out of the wrong state which
is deep slow wave sleep,

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:01.410
is because--

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:04.680
I liken it to like a
washing machine cycle.

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:07.690
This 90-minute cycle is like
a washing machine cycle.

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:10.890
And the first part is
to add water, right?

00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:13.250
And then your clothes
are soaking wet.

00:38:13.250 --> 00:38:15.000
You don't want to open
the washing machine

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:18.488
and try and function, put
them on, and wear them around

00:38:18.488 --> 00:38:20.280
while they're soaking
wet and full of soap.

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:23.310
So you have to wait until
the cycle is through

00:38:23.310 --> 00:38:27.150
before you can, well, actually,
let's put it in the dryer,

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:30.210
too, before you
want to wear them.

00:38:30.210 --> 00:38:34.050
So yeah, you can function.

00:38:34.050 --> 00:38:36.690
It just takes a little
while for those clothes,

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:39.930
that brain to dry out, so you
can actually function well.

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:43.660
But it's better to wait through
the whole cycle is complete.

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:46.950
And so that's why
you want to set

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:49.500
that 90-minute, alarm clock.

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:51.750
And again, that's
around 90 minutes

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:55.350
because the first stage of
sleep, the first cycle of sleep

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:59.790
is actually a little longer,
more like 105, 110, minutes.

00:38:59.790 --> 00:39:01.500
But then the second
ones and third ones,

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:04.200
they get shorter and shorter
as the night goes on.

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:06.180
And in the last
few cycles, you're

00:39:06.180 --> 00:39:10.770
just doing the end to REM sleep
cycle which takes less time.

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:12.270
And if you wake up
out of REM sleep,

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:14.670
there's usually no
problem cognitively.

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:16.650
You're good to go.

00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:20.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Are you
a fan of sleep trackers?

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:21.395
GINA POE: Sure, yeah.

00:39:21.395 --> 00:39:22.728
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you use one?

00:39:22.728 --> 00:39:23.760
GINA POE: I have one on.

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:27.010
I don't take--

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:30.580
I don't live my life
by them, because they

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:33.220
are-- the best ones
right now are about 70%

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:35.530
effective at staging your sleep.

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:38.410
So 70%, it's OK, it's OK.

00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:40.643
But take it with a grain
of salt is what I'm saying.

00:39:40.643 --> 00:39:41.560
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:42.560
I've tried various ones.

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:45.040
And I compare the
mattress based one to--

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:47.430
actually wear it on my
ankle instead of my wrist.

00:39:47.430 --> 00:39:49.030
And I do find it informative.

00:39:49.030 --> 00:39:51.670
But a colleague of
mine at Stanford,

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:55.480
Ali Crum, who works on mindset
and belief effects talked

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:57.940
to me about a study they
did where people often

00:39:57.940 --> 00:40:01.450
will bias their sense
of daytime wakefulness

00:40:01.450 --> 00:40:05.145
based on their sleep score more
than their subjective score.

00:40:05.145 --> 00:40:06.520
In other words,
if they were told

00:40:06.520 --> 00:40:07.870
they got a poor night's
sleep, even if they

00:40:07.870 --> 00:40:09.190
got a great night's sleep--

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:11.230
and this was of course
measured in the sleep lab

00:40:11.230 --> 00:40:13.240
so they were able
to compare-- people

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:14.860
report feeling more groggy.

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:18.210
And the opposite is also true
that if it says 100% or 90%

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:19.960
on your sleep score,
then people go, oh, I

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:21.950
feel great, even though they
might not have slept well.

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:24.400
So this speaks to the, I don't
want to say placebo effect,

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:26.920
but the belief effects that
are woven in with a score.

00:40:26.920 --> 00:40:27.548
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:40:27.548 --> 00:40:28.090
That's right.

00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:29.507
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So it seems to me

00:40:29.507 --> 00:40:31.960
that combining subjective
and objective data

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:32.860
is probably best.

00:40:32.860 --> 00:40:36.460
GINA POE: And I do believe
that you should trust your own

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:40.720
physiology and the way that
your body is telling you to feel

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:45.220
because in fact, it used to
be that people with insomnia

00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:45.790
weren't--

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:48.880
were often not believed because
you put them in a sleep lab,

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:50.380
and they look like
they slept great.

00:40:50.380 --> 00:40:51.590
And you wake them
up in the morning,

00:40:51.590 --> 00:40:53.780
they say, oh, I didn't
sleep very well at all.

00:40:53.780 --> 00:40:56.290
And that's because
probably we just came out

00:40:56.290 --> 00:40:59.920
with a paper that shows that
subcortical structures can

00:40:59.920 --> 00:41:01.840
be in a completely
different sleep state

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:04.960
than cortical structures which
is what we measure in the sleep

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:06.282
lab what the cortex is doing.

00:41:06.282 --> 00:41:07.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:41:07.490 --> 00:41:09.220
GINA POE: So it
might be that people

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:11.560
who say, I did not sleep
all night long even

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:14.600
though the cortex is saying,
oh, no, you had great sleep,

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:19.150
was because they're
monitoring their subcortical,

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:24.310
hypothalamus, hippocampus,
thalamus, other structures

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:27.460
that the sleep lab
just can't access

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:30.670
unless you have depth electrodes
which nobody really wants.

00:41:30.670 --> 00:41:31.628
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:41:31.628 --> 00:41:34.280
Because that requires holes
in the skull and wires.

00:41:34.280 --> 00:41:34.780
Wow.

00:41:34.780 --> 00:41:39.190
So does that mean that the last
50 plus years of sleep science

00:41:39.190 --> 00:41:41.620
is potentially
flawed in some way,

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:43.485
because they're only
recording from--

00:41:43.485 --> 00:41:44.860
I guess this would
be the analogy

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:47.290
would be-- it's like
recording from the surface

00:41:47.290 --> 00:41:49.833
of the ocean as opposed
to the depth of the ocean.

00:41:49.833 --> 00:41:50.500
GINA POE: Right.

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:54.997
And trying to ascertain the life
moving down deep in the depth--

00:41:54.997 --> 00:41:56.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Brace
yourselves colleagues

00:41:56.830 --> 00:41:58.600
at Stanford sleep
lab and elsewhere.

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:00.880
But please just tell us
because I think scientists

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:02.050
want to know the truth.

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:02.980
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:05.200
It's not for nothing
that you want

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:06.820
to know what the
cortex is doing.

00:42:06.820 --> 00:42:09.260
The cortex is really
important for a lot of things.

00:42:09.260 --> 00:42:11.140
But it doesn't
necessarily tell you

00:42:11.140 --> 00:42:13.840
what a lot of other really
important parts of the brain

00:42:13.840 --> 00:42:17.350
are doing in terms of sleep.

00:42:17.350 --> 00:42:21.440
But there's hope, because
in fact, it would be great.

00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:24.550
I think that's possible, from
the paper if you look at it,

00:42:24.550 --> 00:42:30.430
it's in PNAS this year, that
you could detect subtle changes

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:32.710
in the cortical
EEG that might be

00:42:32.710 --> 00:42:35.320
able to tell you what the
subcortical structures are

00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:35.980
doing.

00:42:35.980 --> 00:42:39.460
Things like the absolute
power in that sleep

00:42:39.460 --> 00:42:43.090
spindle band, that
sigma band would change

00:42:43.090 --> 00:42:46.960
if the hippocampus is in
REM sleep and the cortex

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:50.060
is in that sleep spindle
state and vise versa.

00:42:50.060 --> 00:42:53.380
So there is some
hope that we can

00:42:53.380 --> 00:42:57.130
gain from people with
depth electrodes or animals

00:42:57.130 --> 00:43:01.630
with depth electrodes that we
could backwards machine learn

00:43:01.630 --> 00:43:04.810
what the cortex might
be able to tell us

00:43:04.810 --> 00:43:07.892
about subcortical structures
from the cortical EEG, so.

00:43:07.892 --> 00:43:09.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:43:09.100 --> 00:43:11.628
This is going to be a
stimu-- yes, stay tuned.

00:43:11.628 --> 00:43:14.170
It's going to be a stimulus for
development of new technology

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:19.000
which is always going to
assist in scientific discovery.

00:43:19.000 --> 00:43:20.980
There is one more
thing I wanted to ask

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:24.400
about the architecture
of the night sleep

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:26.470
in terms of early
part of the night.

00:43:26.470 --> 00:43:29.170
Earlier, you
mentioned the washout

00:43:29.170 --> 00:43:32.410
of debris and the
so-called lymphatic system,

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:33.910
I think is what
you're referring to.

00:43:33.910 --> 00:43:35.368
Could you tell us
a little bit more

00:43:35.368 --> 00:43:38.425
about the washout that occurs
in the brain during sleep,

00:43:38.425 --> 00:43:41.830
what that is, and what
roles it's thought to serve,

00:43:41.830 --> 00:43:44.830
and perhaps if there are any
ways to ensure that it happens

00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:46.810
or to ensure that
it doesn't happen,

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:48.580
and obviously we
want this to happen?

00:43:48.580 --> 00:43:50.260
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:51.970
All right, great question.

00:43:51.970 --> 00:43:53.770
We talked about
the circadian clock

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:56.140
and how certain things
happen at certain times.

00:43:56.140 --> 00:43:57.640
Well, one of the
things that happens

00:43:57.640 --> 00:44:00.160
when we're awake and
talking to each other

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:03.070
is that there's a
lot of plasticity.

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:05.380
There's something that I'm
learning from you today.

00:44:05.380 --> 00:44:06.850
And you're learning from me.

00:44:06.850 --> 00:44:09.340
And that changes
our synapses, and it

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:14.380
changes the way our proteins are
going to be folded and changed

00:44:14.380 --> 00:44:16.520
during sleep.

00:44:16.520 --> 00:44:17.410
It unfolds.

00:44:17.410 --> 00:44:22.420
This process actually uses a
lot of ATP, the power structure

00:44:22.420 --> 00:44:23.860
the fuel of the brain.

00:44:23.860 --> 00:44:28.090
And it unfolds also proteins
while we're doing this,

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:29.690
while we're using them.

00:44:29.690 --> 00:44:33.040
And so during that first part
of the night, when we first

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:35.320
fall asleep in the
first 20 minutes or so,

00:44:35.320 --> 00:44:39.460
we're building that
adenosine back into ATP.

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:41.890
And that's probably
why power naps

00:44:41.890 --> 00:44:44.350
are called power naps,
because we're actually

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:45.790
rebuilding the power.

00:44:45.790 --> 00:44:51.418
And then we're also cleaning
out through the deep slow waves

00:44:51.418 --> 00:44:52.210
of slow wave sleep.

00:44:52.210 --> 00:44:54.850
We're cleaning out all
those misfolded proteins,

00:44:54.850 --> 00:44:58.180
unfolded proteins,
and other things that

00:44:58.180 --> 00:45:03.040
get broken down and
need to be rebuilt

00:45:03.040 --> 00:45:07.700
when we're asleep because of
its use during wakefulness.

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:11.740
So I liken that to having a
big party during wakefulness.

00:45:11.740 --> 00:45:14.200
And you need all those
partygoers to leave in order

00:45:14.200 --> 00:45:15.250
to do the cleanup.

00:45:15.250 --> 00:45:18.250
And so what I think the
mechanism is, and this is still

00:45:18.250 --> 00:45:19.780
something to be
tested, is actually

00:45:19.780 --> 00:45:22.863
slow waves themselves
which is bad news for us

00:45:22.863 --> 00:45:23.530
as we get older.

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:25.300
And those slow
waves get smaller.

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:29.830
And so your sleep goes away.

00:45:29.830 --> 00:45:34.510
So what happens when a neuron
is firing is that it expands,

00:45:34.510 --> 00:45:37.390
the membrane expands a little
bit, becomes more translucent.

00:45:37.390 --> 00:45:38.890
That's how we know--
one of the ways

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:41.650
we know that neurons
expand when they fire.

00:45:41.650 --> 00:45:44.500
And so every action
potential, the membrane

00:45:44.500 --> 00:45:48.700
expands a little bit, as sodium
brings water into the cell.

00:45:48.700 --> 00:45:51.410
And then when they're
silent, they contract.

00:45:51.410 --> 00:45:53.890
And so during slow
waves, the cool thing

00:45:53.890 --> 00:45:56.080
is that the reason why
you can measure them

00:45:56.080 --> 00:45:58.300
is that all the neurons
at the same time--

00:45:58.300 --> 00:46:00.850
not all of them but a
good portion of them--

00:46:00.850 --> 00:46:03.740
are firing at the same time
and silent at the same time.

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:07.300
And so you think about that
as contracting and expanding

00:46:07.300 --> 00:46:08.650
all at the same time.

00:46:08.650 --> 00:46:11.000
It's like a bilge
pump of the brain.

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:12.400
So that can pump out--

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:15.600
glia are also really
important for this

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:19.720
in terms of cleaning up
debris and transferring it

00:46:19.720 --> 00:46:21.910
to where it needs to go.

00:46:21.910 --> 00:46:23.980
So I think of it
actually as a bilge

00:46:23.980 --> 00:46:26.312
pump cleaning out our brain.

00:46:26.312 --> 00:46:27.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:46:27.520 --> 00:46:30.280
I've heard about the lymphatic
system and lymphatic wash out.

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:32.740
I've never thought about
the mechanical aspects of it

00:46:32.740 --> 00:46:33.250
before.

00:46:33.250 --> 00:46:36.670
I always thought that for
some reason that now it's

00:46:36.670 --> 00:46:39.820
obvious to me, there had to be
something mechanical but only

00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:41.950
now that you've
educated me about this.

00:46:41.950 --> 00:46:45.070
I thought that for some reason
the cerebral spinal fluid just

00:46:45.070 --> 00:46:46.220
starts washing through.

00:46:46.220 --> 00:46:48.730
But here, you're talking
about literally an expansion

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:51.070
and a contraction of
the neurons in unison

00:46:51.070 --> 00:46:54.250
and pushing the fluid
through, cleaning out

00:46:54.250 --> 00:46:56.470
any misfolded proteins
or debris that

00:46:56.470 --> 00:46:59.890
might occur on the basis of
these metabolic pathways.

00:46:59.890 --> 00:47:03.010
And the consequence
of that is to, what,

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:09.325
to leave the brain in a
state of more pristine action

00:47:09.325 --> 00:47:10.160
for the next day.

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:10.660
is?

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:10.990
That right?

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:13.157
GINA POE: Yeah you think
of it, again, like a party.

00:47:13.157 --> 00:47:14.940
And if you don't clean
up after that party

00:47:14.940 --> 00:47:17.080
and you try and hold
another one the next day,

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:19.420
it's going to get more clogged.

00:47:19.420 --> 00:47:22.720
People have a harder time moving
around and enjoying themselves.

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:25.600
And if that builds
up day after day,

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:27.490
it's going to be cognition.

00:47:27.490 --> 00:47:30.490
That would be the
partygoers moving around

00:47:30.490 --> 00:47:33.790
becomes hard, yeah.

00:47:33.790 --> 00:47:35.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And so this bilge

00:47:35.320 --> 00:47:36.910
pump that you
describe is associated

00:47:36.910 --> 00:47:41.380
with the big slow
waves of deep-- well,

00:47:41.380 --> 00:47:43.265
of slow wave sleep.

00:47:43.265 --> 00:47:45.640
So this is going to occur more
or less in the first third

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:46.780
of the night, is that right?

00:47:46.780 --> 00:47:47.738
GINA POE: That's right.

00:47:47.738 --> 00:47:49.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And are there things

00:47:49.300 --> 00:47:52.090
that inhibit this process
and are there things

00:47:52.090 --> 00:47:53.980
that facilitate this process?

00:47:53.980 --> 00:47:55.990
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:47:55.990 --> 00:47:59.742
So well, one thing to inhibit
is not to get it but--

00:47:59.742 --> 00:48:00.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:48:00.700 --> 00:48:03.400
And here, too-- sorry
to interrupt but--

00:48:03.400 --> 00:48:06.070
and is this similar to the
case with growth hormone

00:48:06.070 --> 00:48:09.160
where if you go to sleep
later than you would normally,

00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:10.635
you miss the washout?

00:48:10.635 --> 00:48:11.260
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:48:11.260 --> 00:48:12.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
not you don't delay it.

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:14.035
You miss-- you
missed the washout.

00:48:14.035 --> 00:48:14.560
GINA POE: That's right.

00:48:14.560 --> 00:48:15.190
That's right.

00:48:15.190 --> 00:48:18.160
So if you go to sleep at
1:00 or 2:00 in the morning,

00:48:18.160 --> 00:48:20.830
your sleep is still going
to be dominated by N2,

00:48:20.830 --> 00:48:23.320
NREM sleep not by
slow wave sleep.

00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:25.090
So you need to--

00:48:25.090 --> 00:48:27.100
you need to get that
first bit of sleep.

00:48:27.100 --> 00:48:28.725
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Would a caveat to that

00:48:28.725 --> 00:48:31.060
be if somebody normally goes
to sleep at 1:00 or 2:00

00:48:31.060 --> 00:48:34.810
AM and wakes up at 10:00 AM
if that's their normal sleep

00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:35.320
cycle--

00:48:35.320 --> 00:48:36.520
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:48:36.520 --> 00:48:37.870
That should be OK.

00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:40.390
It should be OK.

00:48:40.390 --> 00:48:42.490
You would probably want to do--

00:48:42.490 --> 00:48:44.770
somebody would want to do
a sleep study with people

00:48:44.770 --> 00:48:48.190
who do that normally and see if
also the melatonin release is

00:48:48.190 --> 00:48:52.000
later and the corticosterone
rise that happens normally

00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:54.170
in the morning
also happens later.

00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:56.867
So if everything shifted, good.

00:48:56.867 --> 00:48:57.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:48:57.700 --> 00:48:58.200
Yeah.

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:00.430
There are a few
studies I've come

00:49:00.430 --> 00:49:05.020
across that really do argue
for the fact that waking up

00:49:05.020 --> 00:49:08.800
circa sunrise, that doesn't mean
at sunrise but within an hour

00:49:08.800 --> 00:49:10.540
or two, maybe three
hours of sunrise

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:15.310
and going to sleep within
four hours after sunset

00:49:15.310 --> 00:49:20.917
or so is actually better for
the health of all human beings

00:49:20.917 --> 00:49:22.000
than is being a night owl.

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:23.380
And the night owl,
there's almost

00:49:23.380 --> 00:49:26.103
like a night owl posse
out there, especially

00:49:26.103 --> 00:49:26.770
on social media.

00:49:26.770 --> 00:49:29.200
They get very upset when
you say that you should

00:49:29.200 --> 00:49:31.930
see morning sunlight
that after 10:00 AM,

00:49:31.930 --> 00:49:33.670
you kind of miss the boat.

00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:35.560
They get very upset,
because I think

00:49:35.560 --> 00:49:39.320
there are about 20% or
30% of people perhaps

00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:41.700
who really feel like they
function better staying up

00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:42.700
late and waking up late.

00:49:42.700 --> 00:49:45.672
And they function much
less well waking up early

00:49:45.672 --> 00:49:46.630
and going to bed early.

00:49:46.630 --> 00:49:50.560
But the data on health metrics
suggests that, sorry night

00:49:50.560 --> 00:49:52.388
owls, that they are wrong.

00:49:52.388 --> 00:49:54.430
GINA POE: Yeah, sorry me
because I'm a night owl.

00:49:54.430 --> 00:49:54.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, boy.

00:49:54.850 --> 00:49:55.350
OK.

00:49:55.350 --> 00:49:56.890
Well, then I'm
apologizing directly.

00:49:56.890 --> 00:49:59.300
Here, I'm not a really
early morning person.

00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:00.430
I'm more typical.

00:50:00.430 --> 00:50:04.240
I wake up naturally around 6:30,
somewhere between 6:30 and 7:30

00:50:04.240 --> 00:50:09.550
AM, go to sleep somewhere
between 10:00 and 11:00 PM.

00:50:09.550 --> 00:50:11.530
These are averages.

00:50:11.530 --> 00:50:13.420
But I do notice
that when I force

00:50:13.420 --> 00:50:14.890
myself to get up
a little earlier

00:50:14.890 --> 00:50:19.720
and go to sleep a little
earlier, that my mood

00:50:19.720 --> 00:50:22.670
and alertness and just overall
productivity is much higher.

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:24.670
And there could be other
variables to that, too.

00:50:24.670 --> 00:50:25.540
GINA POE: You're
absolutely right.

00:50:25.540 --> 00:50:26.720
I'm a night owl.

00:50:26.720 --> 00:50:29.290
I love staying up late
at night doing you know,

00:50:29.290 --> 00:50:32.115
writing grants, writing
papers, watching movies,

00:50:32.115 --> 00:50:32.740
whatever it is.

00:50:32.740 --> 00:50:34.030
I love it.

00:50:34.030 --> 00:50:38.470
But like you and like
every human being on earth

00:50:38.470 --> 00:50:42.230
would do better if I go to bed
earlier and wake up earlier.

00:50:42.230 --> 00:50:44.920
So one good thing
for night owls is

00:50:44.920 --> 00:50:47.770
to have a child, because
they will wake you up.

00:50:47.770 --> 00:50:50.590
Their circadian
rhythms are so strong.

00:50:50.590 --> 00:50:51.550
They will wake up.

00:50:51.550 --> 00:50:53.230
And even if you
deprive them of sleep

00:50:53.230 --> 00:50:55.150
in the first half
of the night they

00:50:55.150 --> 00:50:57.100
will still wake
up like clockwork,

00:50:57.100 --> 00:51:00.010
because their circadian rhythms
are so strong at 6:00 AM.

00:51:00.010 --> 00:51:02.530
And so what you've-- you
haven't done anything good

00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:03.250
for your kid.

00:51:03.250 --> 00:51:06.040
You haven't moved their cycle
to later and be more in line

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:06.670
with yours.

00:51:06.670 --> 00:51:08.500
In fact, you've just
sleep deprived them

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:11.073
and made them miss a window and
made them cranky the next day

00:51:11.073 --> 00:51:12.490
and made your life
more miserable.

00:51:12.490 --> 00:51:16.060
So go to bed soon after
your kids go to bed and wake

00:51:16.060 --> 00:51:16.670
up with them.

00:51:16.670 --> 00:51:17.710
That's the way to do it.

00:51:17.710 --> 00:51:17.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:20.290
The child alarm clock, another
reason to have children.

00:51:20.290 --> 00:51:21.130
GINA POE: Yes.

00:51:21.130 --> 00:51:23.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
got a dog, a puppy,

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:26.830
and then that became a
dog, specifically, well,

00:51:26.830 --> 00:51:27.770
for many reasons.

00:51:27.770 --> 00:51:30.270
But one reason was I wanted to
be one of those early morning

00:51:30.270 --> 00:51:32.170
rides, 5:30 AM every morning.

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:34.600
But I ended up getting a
bulldog that would literally

00:51:34.600 --> 00:51:36.940
sleep 16 hours if he could.

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:40.190
A nuclear bomb could go off,
and he wouldn't wake up.

00:51:40.190 --> 00:51:43.300
But what I started to learn
was that Bulldogs actually

00:51:43.300 --> 00:51:44.970
have sleep apnea.

00:51:44.970 --> 00:51:46.720
As far as I know,
they're the only species

00:51:46.720 --> 00:51:49.570
that has a genetically--

00:51:49.570 --> 00:51:52.360
they're essentially an
inbred sleep defect.

00:51:52.360 --> 00:51:53.950
And so I actually
don't encourage

00:51:53.950 --> 00:51:56.410
people to get bulldogs because
it's kind of a cruel breed.

00:51:56.410 --> 00:51:58.810
They suffer a lot in that
body that they're born into.

00:51:58.810 --> 00:52:02.350
Anyway, a dog can
accomplish some of this.

00:52:02.350 --> 00:52:06.183
But get the breed of dog that
is going to wake up early.

00:52:06.183 --> 00:52:08.350
So in other words, don't
get a bulldog or a mastiff.

00:52:08.350 --> 00:52:12.970
GINA POE: Well, interestingly,
all predatory animals

00:52:12.970 --> 00:52:16.540
like dogs, and cats,
and lions, and us--

00:52:16.540 --> 00:52:19.630
well, more dogs, cats,
and lions, then us,

00:52:19.630 --> 00:52:23.200
well, can sleep 16 hours a day.

00:52:23.200 --> 00:52:24.520
Ferrets are predatory.

00:52:24.520 --> 00:52:26.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
had a pet ferret.

00:52:26.020 --> 00:52:28.240
I used to-- and
sadly, I also used

00:52:28.240 --> 00:52:30.310
to work on ferrets,
published a number of papers,

00:52:30.310 --> 00:52:31.155
delightful animals.

00:52:31.155 --> 00:52:31.780
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:52:31.780 --> 00:52:34.480
And great because you
can study development.

00:52:34.480 --> 00:52:36.280
It's really cool,
because they're

00:52:36.280 --> 00:52:39.430
born very artricial like
we are with brains that

00:52:39.430 --> 00:52:40.810
are not very well developed.

00:52:40.810 --> 00:52:43.660
And so you can see what
happens through development

00:52:43.660 --> 00:52:46.690
and how important these
different phases of development

00:52:46.690 --> 00:52:47.680
really are.

00:52:47.680 --> 00:52:52.750
But yes, yeah, maybe we're
not as much predators

00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:55.840
as we think because in
fact, our sleep is somewhere

00:52:55.840 --> 00:52:59.740
between the prey
and the predators

00:52:59.740 --> 00:53:02.680
in terms of the amount of sleep
that we usually need a night.

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:07.690
But those predators can sleep
16 hours, napping all day long.

00:53:07.690 --> 00:53:09.820
And they're more
crepuscular, perhaps,

00:53:09.820 --> 00:53:10.960
like their prey are, more--

00:53:10.960 --> 00:53:12.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
dawn and dusk active.

00:53:12.790 --> 00:53:15.820
GINA POE: Yeah, dawn
and dusk active.

00:53:15.820 --> 00:53:16.660
Yeah.

00:53:16.660 --> 00:53:20.050
But anyway, yes,
children and dogs--

00:53:20.050 --> 00:53:22.930
actually, there was a poll
done by the National Sleep

00:53:22.930 --> 00:53:25.420
Foundation to see what
the number one thing is

00:53:25.420 --> 00:53:26.830
that wakes people up at night.

00:53:26.830 --> 00:53:29.650
And number two is
going to the bathroom.

00:53:29.650 --> 00:53:32.522
Number three is
children, because--

00:53:32.522 --> 00:53:33.730
when your children are young.

00:53:33.730 --> 00:53:36.340
But that only lasts a few
years that they'll wake you up

00:53:36.340 --> 00:53:38.110
when they're babies.

00:53:38.110 --> 00:53:40.000
But the number
one thing is pets.

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:43.360
And pets needing
to go out or cats

00:53:43.360 --> 00:53:45.100
wanting to curl up
with you or whatever

00:53:45.100 --> 00:53:47.500
it is, pets' needs
will wake you up more

00:53:47.500 --> 00:53:49.450
in the middle of the
night than anything else.

00:53:49.450 --> 00:53:52.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Another reason
to not get a nocturnal pet.

00:53:52.330 --> 00:53:54.433
People who get
hamsters, pretty quickly

00:53:54.433 --> 00:53:56.350
realize that they are
nocturnal, and they want

00:53:56.350 --> 00:53:57.480
to run on their wheel around.

00:53:57.480 --> 00:53:58.750
GINA POE: Yeah, you got to
put them in the living room

00:53:58.750 --> 00:54:00.160
away from where you sleep.

00:54:00.160 --> 00:54:03.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I vote fish
tank, folks, freshwater fish

00:54:03.150 --> 00:54:03.650
tank.

00:54:03.650 --> 00:54:06.108
There are all sorts of reasons
to not get a salt water fish

00:54:06.108 --> 00:54:08.560
tank or a child.

00:54:08.560 --> 00:54:09.760
[LAUGHS]

00:54:09.760 --> 00:54:10.763
I appreciate that vote.

00:54:10.763 --> 00:54:12.430
And I appreciate you
mentioning ferrets.

00:54:12.430 --> 00:54:14.222
And by the way, folks,
they are carnivores.

00:54:14.222 --> 00:54:15.100
They are not rodents.

00:54:15.100 --> 00:54:18.610
And they are-- they have very
elaborate brain structures.

00:54:18.610 --> 00:54:22.750
They're very smart, in the same
family as the honey badgers

00:54:22.750 --> 00:54:23.830
and the other mustelids.

00:54:23.830 --> 00:54:27.220
Anyway, I shouldn't geek out
too much on the mustelids

00:54:27.220 --> 00:54:31.060
or else I'll take the
remainder of all our time.

00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:34.270
I'd love for you to
tell us about REM sleep

00:54:34.270 --> 00:54:36.640
and the sleep later in
the night as it relates

00:54:36.640 --> 00:54:38.290
to dreams and emotionality.

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:41.230
And this is probably the
appropriate time for you

00:54:41.230 --> 00:54:43.420
to introduce us to this
incredible structure

00:54:43.420 --> 00:54:48.400
in the brain, which is the locus
coeruleus a difficult structure

00:54:48.400 --> 00:54:53.770
to spell but a beautiful-- a
beautifully named structure.

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:56.260
I find locus coeruleus
to be just fascinating.

00:54:56.260 --> 00:54:59.292
And I know a small
fraction of what it does.

00:54:59.292 --> 00:55:01.000
And I'm hoping you're
going to educate me

00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:04.660
and our audience more about
what it does and hopefully

00:55:04.660 --> 00:55:06.460
tell us a little bit
about its relationship

00:55:06.460 --> 00:55:08.740
to epinephrine a.k.a.

00:55:08.740 --> 00:55:09.310
adrenaline.

00:55:09.310 --> 00:55:09.940
GINA POE: Yeah.

00:55:09.940 --> 00:55:11.920
I am so glad you
brought this up,

00:55:11.920 --> 00:55:14.245
because I can totally geek
out on the locus coeruleus.

00:55:14.245 --> 00:55:15.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Please do.

00:55:15.370 --> 00:55:18.160
GINA POE: Locus, meaning
spot or place and coeruleus,

00:55:18.160 --> 00:55:20.608
meaning blue so you could
just call it the blue spot.

00:55:20.608 --> 00:55:21.400
That's the easiest.

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:27.430
Every animal with a brain
has a blue spot and--

00:55:27.430 --> 00:55:28.930
yeah, and I mean,
every other animal

00:55:28.930 --> 00:55:31.240
with a brain because
of course, there

00:55:31.240 --> 00:55:33.580
are animals with nervous
systems that are not

00:55:33.580 --> 00:55:36.040
centralized like jellyfish.

00:55:36.040 --> 00:55:39.040
But anyway, we're
digressing there.

00:55:39.040 --> 00:55:41.860
So the local coeruleus
is filled with neurons

00:55:41.860 --> 00:55:43.960
that have in them
norepinephrine which

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:47.320
is the brain's version of
epinephrine or adrenaline,

00:55:47.320 --> 00:55:48.970
is also called noradrenaline.

00:55:48.970 --> 00:55:51.790
And what it does is,
just like adrenaline

00:55:51.790 --> 00:55:53.290
and the rest of our
bodies, it helps

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:56.810
prime us to respond
to our environment.

00:55:56.810 --> 00:56:01.390
So when locus coeruleus neurons
fire and fire in a burst,

00:56:01.390 --> 00:56:03.280
we can switch our attention.

00:56:03.280 --> 00:56:06.760
And they will fire in a burst
if, for example, a loud noise

00:56:06.760 --> 00:56:09.310
happens in the middle of your
concentrating on something.

00:56:09.310 --> 00:56:11.590
So you can-- it
helps-- it fires.

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:13.840
And it helps you switch
your attention to that thing

00:56:13.840 --> 00:56:15.530
and then learn quickly from it.

00:56:15.530 --> 00:56:18.040
So it's really important
in a stress response.

00:56:18.040 --> 00:56:21.970
It helps us do quick
one trial learning.

00:56:21.970 --> 00:56:24.640
And then tonic
activity during the day

00:56:24.640 --> 00:56:27.520
when you're just
doing normal going

00:56:27.520 --> 00:56:31.660
about your normal concentration
activities is really

00:56:31.660 --> 00:56:35.220
good for sustained attention.

00:56:35.220 --> 00:56:39.158
It works with the cholinergic
system of our basal forebrain

00:56:39.158 --> 00:56:41.200
which is really important
for learning and memory

00:56:41.200 --> 00:56:46.990
also to help us learn about
things and put things together.

00:56:46.990 --> 00:56:50.170
But just tonic
levels are signature

00:56:50.170 --> 00:56:52.340
of wakefulness and alertness.

00:56:52.340 --> 00:56:56.380
So too much is panic with
a locus coeruleus activity.

00:56:56.380 --> 00:56:59.140
A burst is switching attention.

00:56:59.140 --> 00:57:03.190
And then tonic levels are
sustained constant attention.

00:57:03.190 --> 00:57:05.650
And then when we go to
sleep, the locus coeruleus

00:57:05.650 --> 00:57:10.030
slows and goes from
about on average two

00:57:10.030 --> 00:57:15.520
Hertz to about one Hertz, one
cycle per second, tonically.

00:57:15.520 --> 00:57:17.020
And then when we
go into REM sleep,

00:57:17.020 --> 00:57:19.390
it's the only time when
it shuts off completely.

00:57:19.390 --> 00:57:23.200
And it appears that,
that complete silence

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:26.360
is really, really important
for a number of things.

00:57:26.360 --> 00:57:29.260
And the main thing that I
think it's important for

00:57:29.260 --> 00:57:34.030
is the ability to erase and
break down synapses that

00:57:34.030 --> 00:57:35.320
are no longer working for us.

00:57:35.320 --> 00:57:38.320
So they encode things
that are false now,

00:57:38.320 --> 00:57:43.330
or they are encoding things
that we learned in the novelty,

00:57:43.330 --> 00:57:48.490
encoding pathway of our brain
that have now been consolidated

00:57:48.490 --> 00:57:49.613
to other pathways.

00:57:49.613 --> 00:57:52.030
And so we need to now erase
them from the novelty encoding

00:57:52.030 --> 00:57:52.720
pathway.

00:57:52.720 --> 00:57:54.670
And that is really,
really important

00:57:54.670 --> 00:57:59.150
for being able to continue to
learn things all of our lives,

00:57:59.150 --> 00:58:02.560
so like erasing
that REM or that--

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:05.522
what you call those, disks that
you stick into computers that--

00:58:05.522 --> 00:58:06.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Hard drive--

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:08.230
no, thumb drives.

00:58:08.230 --> 00:58:10.030
GINA POE: Yeah, erasing
your thumb drive.

00:58:10.030 --> 00:58:13.060
So that thumb drive is what
you carry around all day long.

00:58:13.060 --> 00:58:16.870
And then during sleep, you write
that thumb drive to the cortex,

00:58:16.870 --> 00:58:18.610
to the long term
memory structures.

00:58:18.610 --> 00:58:20.800
And you need to refresh
that thumb drive.

00:58:20.800 --> 00:58:22.900
And that's what happens
during REM sleep

00:58:22.900 --> 00:58:25.480
when the locus coeruleus is
off, because whenever it's on

00:58:25.480 --> 00:58:27.310
and noradrenaline
is there, it helps

00:58:27.310 --> 00:58:28.810
us to put things together.

00:58:28.810 --> 00:58:31.090
It helps us to learn
and strengthen synapses.

00:58:31.090 --> 00:58:34.060
But it does not allow
us to actually weaken

00:58:34.060 --> 00:58:37.370
synapses that are also a really
important part for life--

00:58:37.370 --> 00:58:40.630
important part of
lifelong learning.

00:58:40.630 --> 00:58:42.893
Yeah, so much more I
could say about that.

00:58:42.893 --> 00:58:45.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, locus
coeruleus sounds fascinating.

00:58:45.310 --> 00:58:49.780
So it's connected to the basal
forebrain cholinergic system.

00:58:49.780 --> 00:58:52.870
The neurons in locus
coeruleus, if I'm not mistaken,

00:58:52.870 --> 00:58:58.850
release norepinephrine,
perhaps epinephrine as well?

00:58:58.850 --> 00:59:01.370
GINA POE: Well, no, the
brain's version of epinephrine

00:59:01.370 --> 00:59:02.600
is norepinephrine.

00:59:02.600 --> 00:59:04.790
The other thing it also--

00:59:04.790 --> 00:59:07.430
the precursor to
norepinephrine is dopamine.

00:59:07.430 --> 00:59:11.210
And so the source of
dopamine in the hippocampus

00:59:11.210 --> 00:59:12.620
seems to be the locus coeruleus.

00:59:12.620 --> 00:59:15.620
And it's still a mystery
is under what conditions

00:59:15.620 --> 00:59:17.900
the locus coeruleus
also releases dopamine.

00:59:17.900 --> 00:59:21.110
But it's really important when
we're learning something new

00:59:21.110 --> 00:59:24.020
to also release dopamine
or to at least activate

00:59:24.020 --> 00:59:26.660
the dopaminergic receptors
in our hippocampus.

00:59:26.660 --> 00:59:30.300
So yeah, so dopamine,
norepinephrine,

00:59:30.300 --> 00:59:34.040
and then there's
also galanin which

00:59:34.040 --> 00:59:36.740
is important for releasing
when we're stressed.

00:59:36.740 --> 00:59:40.160
And it helps also
without rapid learning.

00:59:40.160 --> 00:59:42.440
It works in concert
with norepinephrine

00:59:42.440 --> 00:59:45.620
and in doing what it needs to
do to strengthen synapses so

00:59:45.620 --> 00:59:47.600
that we learn really quickly.

00:59:47.600 --> 00:59:49.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I love that there

00:59:49.790 --> 00:59:53.990
are multiple molecules involved,
because that signals us

00:59:53.990 --> 00:59:57.340
to a principle which is that
even if people can't remember

00:59:57.340 --> 01:00:01.430
all the names, that
rarely in biology

01:00:01.430 --> 01:00:04.100
is something handled by just
one molecule or pathway.

01:00:04.100 --> 01:00:09.390
That redundancy is the rule
because signaling attention

01:00:09.390 --> 01:00:11.440
to specific events
is so important.

01:00:11.440 --> 01:00:14.130
So that-- I'm going to
use that as just a story.

01:00:14.130 --> 01:00:17.040
I always say I wasn't
consulted at the design phase.

01:00:17.040 --> 01:00:19.200
But it makes sense to
me as to why redundancy

01:00:19.200 --> 01:00:20.360
would exist in this system.

01:00:20.360 --> 01:00:21.235
GINA POE: Absolutely.

01:00:21.235 --> 01:00:24.250
And in fact, when we form a
hypothesis about the brain,

01:00:24.250 --> 01:00:25.758
we are always wrong.

01:00:25.758 --> 01:00:27.300
And the reason why
we're always wrong

01:00:27.300 --> 01:00:30.150
is because it's more complicated
than we'd like to think.

01:00:30.150 --> 01:00:32.730
And because in our brains
when we're forming hypotheses,

01:00:32.730 --> 01:00:36.090
it's we fail to account
for all of the factors that

01:00:36.090 --> 01:00:38.970
are involved, the glia,
the neuropeptides,

01:00:38.970 --> 01:00:42.960
the neurotransmitters, the
physical structure of synapses.

01:00:42.960 --> 01:00:48.540
And so when I was going through
grad school 35 years ago, we--

01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:51.953
the dogma was that every neuron
contains one neurotransmitter

01:00:51.953 --> 01:00:53.370
and releases one
neurotransmitter.

01:00:53.370 --> 01:00:55.200
And you had excitatory
neurotransmitters,

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:57.120
and inhibitory
neurotransmitters, and neuro--

01:00:57.120 --> 01:00:58.560
modulatory neurotransmitters.

01:00:58.560 --> 01:01:00.480
But that's as
complicated as it's got.

01:01:00.480 --> 01:01:03.120
And then we started talking
about neuropeptides.

01:01:03.120 --> 01:01:06.060
And people said, oh, no,
please don't complicate it.

01:01:06.060 --> 01:01:08.400
And then we started talking
about how neurons contain

01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:10.590
both neuropeptides and
neurotransmitters and maybe

01:01:10.590 --> 01:01:11.610
more than one neurotransmitter--

01:01:11.610 --> 01:01:13.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Maybe
even hormones, too.

01:01:13.360 --> 01:01:16.420
GINA POE: And hormones and-- oh,
Lord, it's just so complicated.

01:01:16.420 --> 01:01:19.110
But I've got to admit
that's why it works, right?

01:01:19.110 --> 01:01:21.960
And every time the brain teaches
us something new about itself

01:01:21.960 --> 01:01:23.760
that we didn't
hypothesize, we say,

01:01:23.760 --> 01:01:27.360
oh, of course, that wouldn't
work if the way I hypothesized

01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:28.110
it-- with it.

01:01:28.110 --> 01:01:29.760
We actually need redundancy.

01:01:29.760 --> 01:01:32.400
We need all of these
systems to work together.

01:01:32.400 --> 01:01:33.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:01:33.540 --> 01:01:35.700
It's daunting
sometimes, but it also

01:01:35.700 --> 01:01:39.760
insures many careers in science
and neuroscience in particular.

01:01:39.760 --> 01:01:43.650
So note that
aspiring scientists,

01:01:43.650 --> 01:01:46.098
there's plenty of
room for discovery.

01:01:46.098 --> 01:01:48.390
GINA POE: Do you want me to
talk about norepinephrine--

01:01:48.390 --> 01:01:48.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:01:48.660 --> 01:01:48.990
So what--

01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:49.770
GINA POE: Emotion and--

01:01:49.770 --> 01:01:50.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yes.

01:01:50.280 --> 01:01:52.620
Well, what I'd love-- what
I'd love for you to tell

01:01:52.620 --> 01:01:57.750
us about is what role this
lack of norepinephrine release

01:01:57.750 --> 01:02:01.890
during rapid eye movement
sleep is thought to achieve?

01:02:01.890 --> 01:02:05.220
And maybe you can also
review some of your work,

01:02:05.220 --> 01:02:08.920
describing conditions under
which norepinephrine invades--

01:02:08.920 --> 01:02:10.170
GINA POE: Yeah, invade sleep.

01:02:10.170 --> 01:02:11.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Rapid eye movement

01:02:11.160 --> 01:02:12.720
sleep and other
patterns of sleep

01:02:12.720 --> 01:02:14.100
and how that can be detrimental.

01:02:14.100 --> 01:02:14.730
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:02:14.730 --> 01:02:17.040
So a lot of this is
hypothetical but based

01:02:17.040 --> 01:02:19.500
on a lot of good evidence
that we're sewing together

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:22.330
into a schema from which
these hypotheses come,

01:02:22.330 --> 01:02:25.690
so a model schema from
which the hypotheses come.

01:02:25.690 --> 01:02:27.720
But one thing that
happens to people

01:02:27.720 --> 01:02:29.880
with post-traumatic
stress disorder

01:02:29.880 --> 01:02:32.430
is that there is
a lot of evidence

01:02:32.430 --> 01:02:36.010
that the locus coeruleus doesn't
stop firing in REM sleep.

01:02:36.010 --> 01:02:39.600
So-- whereas their
levels of norepinephrine

01:02:39.600 --> 01:02:43.080
might be similar to
people without PTSD

01:02:43.080 --> 01:02:46.920
during the day and even during
the first part of the night,

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:49.090
during the wee hours
of the morning.

01:02:49.090 --> 01:02:51.150
And when you measure
norepinephrine levels

01:02:51.150 --> 01:02:55.350
from metabolites in the blood
or the cerebrospinal fluid,

01:02:55.350 --> 01:02:57.582
you see that people
with PTSD, it's

01:02:57.582 --> 01:02:59.040
during the wee
hours of the morning

01:02:59.040 --> 01:03:00.790
when you have the most
REM sleep that they

01:03:00.790 --> 01:03:04.590
have their norepinephrine levels
differentiate most from those

01:03:04.590 --> 01:03:06.300
that don't have PTSD.

01:03:06.300 --> 01:03:09.210
And so that's evidence that
the locus coeruleus is not

01:03:09.210 --> 01:03:11.640
shutting down during REM
sleep like it should.

01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:13.950
Other evidence is
heart rate variability.

01:03:13.950 --> 01:03:17.190
When our locus
coeruleus is firing,

01:03:17.190 --> 01:03:19.620
our heart rates are
generally a little higher.

01:03:19.620 --> 01:03:21.840
And they don't vary
as much as they

01:03:21.840 --> 01:03:25.080
do when the locus
coeruleus is not firing.

01:03:25.080 --> 01:03:26.970
So during slow wave
sleep, normally,

01:03:26.970 --> 01:03:30.090
have this big, juicy variability
in heart rate with every breath

01:03:30.090 --> 01:03:33.360
in and breath out because
our noradrenergic levels

01:03:33.360 --> 01:03:36.540
or norepinephrine levels are
lower during REM sleep that

01:03:36.540 --> 01:03:37.530
goes away entirely.

01:03:37.530 --> 01:03:45.360
And our heart rate is dominated
by parasympathetic rather than

01:03:45.360 --> 01:03:49.080
sympathetic activity and also
what our brain is driving,

01:03:49.080 --> 01:03:50.080
what our dreaming about.

01:03:50.080 --> 01:03:51.955
For example, if we're
dreaming we're running,

01:03:51.955 --> 01:03:53.100
our heart rates will go up.

01:03:53.100 --> 01:03:57.510
But norepinephrine levels
still should be low or off.

01:03:57.510 --> 01:04:02.040
So people with PTSD that
noradrenergic's we're

01:04:02.040 --> 01:04:04.020
studying these in
rats, too, is it true

01:04:04.020 --> 01:04:06.960
that our locus coeruleus
doesn't shut off

01:04:06.960 --> 01:04:09.330
when we have post-traumatic
stress disorder?

01:04:09.330 --> 01:04:11.850
And the preliminary
evidence is yes, it's

01:04:11.850 --> 01:04:13.290
true that it doesn't shut off.

01:04:13.290 --> 01:04:16.980
So what that would do
is norepinephrine would

01:04:16.980 --> 01:04:21.000
act at synapses to prevent
that weakening that you really

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:23.940
need for example of novelty
encoding structures.

01:04:23.940 --> 01:04:27.990
And it keeps memories in that
novelty encoding structure

01:04:27.990 --> 01:04:31.150
even once it's consolidated
to the rest of the brain.

01:04:31.150 --> 01:04:35.160
So in the hippocampus which
is important for remembering

01:04:35.160 --> 01:04:41.175
things throughout our lives
and it's that thumb drive,

01:04:41.175 --> 01:04:43.770
we need it to be
erased so that we

01:04:43.770 --> 01:04:47.130
can learn new things once
it's been consolidated

01:04:47.130 --> 01:04:50.280
to the hard drive of our cortex.

01:04:50.280 --> 01:04:52.890
And so if we're not
able to do that,

01:04:52.890 --> 01:04:56.220
we fill up that
RAM really quickly

01:04:56.220 --> 01:04:57.700
or that thumb drive
really quickly,

01:04:57.700 --> 01:04:59.920
and we're not able
to learn new things.

01:04:59.920 --> 01:05:02.130
So for example,
after a trauma, I

01:05:02.130 --> 01:05:04.140
talked about the locus
coeruleus responding

01:05:04.140 --> 01:05:05.248
in stressful situations.

01:05:05.248 --> 01:05:05.790
That's great.

01:05:05.790 --> 01:05:06.960
It's very adaptive.

01:05:06.960 --> 01:05:08.480
But then you need it to stop.

01:05:08.480 --> 01:05:10.700
Once you've learned what
you need to learn from it

01:05:10.700 --> 01:05:12.658
and you want to go to
sleep, you need the locus

01:05:12.658 --> 01:05:13.910
coeruleus to calm down.

01:05:13.910 --> 01:05:15.680
And during REM sleep,
you want it to stop,

01:05:15.680 --> 01:05:17.930
because then when
you consolidated

01:05:17.930 --> 01:05:19.490
that traumatic
memory to the cortex,

01:05:19.490 --> 01:05:22.340
you need to erase it from the
novelty encoding structures,

01:05:22.340 --> 01:05:25.220
for example, in the
hippocampus so that then when

01:05:25.220 --> 01:05:26.780
you're in the context
of safety, you

01:05:26.780 --> 01:05:29.930
can learn those new
things, those new contexts

01:05:29.930 --> 01:05:35.510
and stop responding
to those same stimuli

01:05:35.510 --> 01:05:38.340
as though you're in
that original situation.

01:05:38.340 --> 01:05:41.730
So if you're not able to
erase that thumb drive,

01:05:41.730 --> 01:05:45.290
you will always feel
like that trauma happened

01:05:45.290 --> 01:05:48.170
that same day,
earlier that same day

01:05:48.170 --> 01:05:53.360
and respond as you
would to an early--

01:05:53.360 --> 01:05:57.870
a recent trauma which is with
beating heart and all of that.

01:05:57.870 --> 01:06:00.950
So even memories
that are years past,

01:06:00.950 --> 01:06:04.670
if you're never able to
downscale that novelty encoding

01:06:04.670 --> 01:06:07.910
structure and purge it
from that traumatic memory,

01:06:07.910 --> 01:06:12.842
it will stay fresh, and new,
and then become maladaptive.

