01:07:31.490 --> 01:07:34.190
What approaches are
you aware of that

01:07:34.190 --> 01:07:38.240
can turn down the output
of locus coeruleus

01:07:38.240 --> 01:07:40.430
during these phases of sleep?

01:07:40.430 --> 01:07:43.310
And for that matter,
what things can

01:07:43.310 --> 01:07:45.200
cause ramping up
of locus coeruleus

01:07:45.200 --> 01:07:47.810
during this phase of sleep?

01:07:47.810 --> 01:07:50.540
We've had a couple of podcast
episodes, solo episodes

01:07:50.540 --> 01:07:52.130
and with guests
talking about trauma.

01:07:52.130 --> 01:07:54.800
We had Dr. Paul Conti who's
a Stanford trained, Harvard

01:07:54.800 --> 01:07:57.590
trained psychiatrist who
talked a lot about trauma

01:07:57.590 --> 01:07:58.970
wrote an excellent
book on trauma

01:07:58.970 --> 01:08:03.782
and certainly sleep was
emphasized as a key thing like

01:08:03.782 --> 01:08:04.490
get enough sleep.

01:08:04.490 --> 01:08:06.657
But here, you're saying
even if somebody with trauma

01:08:06.657 --> 01:08:08.780
gets enough sleep,
if locus coeruleus is

01:08:08.780 --> 01:08:11.575
hyperactive during sleep, those
traumas are going to persist.

01:08:11.575 --> 01:08:13.700
And most of the trauma
treatments that I'm aware of

01:08:13.700 --> 01:08:17.000
are everything ranging from
cognitive behavioral therapy,

01:08:17.000 --> 01:08:20.720
talk therapy, drug
therapy, EMDR, hypnosis.

01:08:20.720 --> 01:08:22.939
Nowadays, there's a lot
of interest and attention

01:08:22.939 --> 01:08:27.319
on clinical studies on exploring
psychedelics, high dose

01:08:27.319 --> 01:08:29.270
psilocybin, and MDMA.

01:08:29.270 --> 01:08:32.700
So it's a vast landscape, none
of which, as far as I know,

01:08:32.700 --> 01:08:34.547
is really focused on
sleep specifically.

01:08:34.547 --> 01:08:35.630
GINA POE: No, they're not.

01:08:35.630 --> 01:08:38.960
And they should be, because
actually, psychedelics

01:08:38.960 --> 01:08:40.430
is a sleep like state.

01:08:40.430 --> 01:08:42.529
And it's a REM sleep like state.

01:08:42.529 --> 01:08:45.240
Although of course, there
are some major differences.

01:08:45.240 --> 01:08:49.020
So yeah, so much
to talk about here.

01:08:49.020 --> 01:08:54.439
So antidepressants are often
noradrenergic or serotonergic

01:08:54.439 --> 01:08:55.760
reuptake inhibitors.

01:08:55.760 --> 01:08:58.189
So they leave
norepinephrine actually

01:08:58.189 --> 01:08:59.600
out there in the synapses.

01:08:59.600 --> 01:09:03.420
And what that does is
it inhibits REM sleep.

01:09:03.420 --> 01:09:04.939
And if you're able
to get REM sleep,

01:09:04.939 --> 01:09:06.800
it would probably
be REM sleep with

01:09:06.800 --> 01:09:08.279
some noradrenergic activity.

01:09:08.279 --> 01:09:10.100
So actually, I think--

01:09:10.100 --> 01:09:13.399
anyway, I'm not a physician--
that antidepressants

01:09:13.399 --> 01:09:14.729
are counter indicated.

01:09:14.729 --> 01:09:17.990
You don't want to take them
if you've experienced a trauma

01:09:17.990 --> 01:09:20.960
and you're experiencing
PTSD, because if anything,

01:09:20.960 --> 01:09:22.850
it's going to make
it worse or at least

01:09:22.850 --> 01:09:24.950
prevent the type of
adaptive REM sleep

01:09:24.950 --> 01:09:28.370
that you really need in order
to resolve those emotions

01:09:28.370 --> 01:09:29.743
and move on.

01:09:29.743 --> 01:09:31.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Is that statement

01:09:31.160 --> 01:09:33.950
specific to
antidepressants that tickle

01:09:33.950 --> 01:09:35.340
the noradrenergic pathway?

01:09:35.340 --> 01:09:38.180
So the one that
comes to mind is--

01:09:38.180 --> 01:09:41.060
can never pronounce it,
buproprion, which is what--

01:09:41.060 --> 01:09:42.770
I think brand name
is Wellbutrin.

01:09:42.770 --> 01:09:46.382
It's a dopaminergic and
noradrenergic agonist.

01:09:46.382 --> 01:09:47.840
So that's the net
effect as opposed

01:09:47.840 --> 01:09:50.899
to the Prozac, Zoloft
variety which are SSRIs.

01:09:50.899 --> 01:09:51.859
GINA POE: Yes, yes.

01:09:51.859 --> 01:09:55.557
But SSRIs themselves
also are problematic,

01:09:55.557 --> 01:09:57.140
because-- we didn't
talk about it yet,

01:09:57.140 --> 01:09:59.330
but the dorsal
raphe nucleus which

01:09:59.330 --> 01:10:03.320
produces serotonin which
specific serotonin-- specific

01:10:03.320 --> 01:10:07.310
serotonin reuptake inhibitors
block from being reuptake

01:10:07.310 --> 01:10:10.190
and leaves too much
serotonin out there.

01:10:10.190 --> 01:10:14.090
And what serotonin also is
another noradrenergic-- sorry,

01:10:14.090 --> 01:10:18.290
another neurotransmitter that's
downregulated during REM sleep.

01:10:18.290 --> 01:10:20.870
That's specifically
off during REM sleep.

01:10:20.870 --> 01:10:25.400
And what serotonin does is it
weights all of our cognition

01:10:25.400 --> 01:10:29.150
to being able to
recognize novelty again.

01:10:29.150 --> 01:10:34.850
So it weights our brain away
from a sense of familiarity

01:10:34.850 --> 01:10:36.170
and toward novelty.

01:10:36.170 --> 01:10:38.900
And it might be
one reason why it's

01:10:38.900 --> 01:10:43.340
an effective antidepressant
because it makes the world feel

01:10:43.340 --> 01:10:45.740
fresh and new again, right?

01:10:45.740 --> 01:10:51.140
But you-- when you
have too much, you're

01:10:51.140 --> 01:10:56.330
holding a novel traumatic
memory in your novelty encoding

01:10:56.330 --> 01:10:59.360
structure too
strongly already, you

01:10:59.360 --> 01:11:02.270
don't want to, again, weight
things toward novelty.

01:11:02.270 --> 01:11:04.130
You need that
absence of serotonin

01:11:04.130 --> 01:11:08.120
also to help you get
that sense of familiarity

01:11:08.120 --> 01:11:11.160
and to start erasing the
novelty encoding structures.

01:11:11.160 --> 01:11:12.532
So you need both to be absent.

01:11:12.532 --> 01:11:14.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
really interesting.

01:11:14.240 --> 01:11:16.010
We hear a lot about serotonin.

01:11:16.010 --> 01:11:20.180
And it's not often discussed in
terms of its features related

01:11:20.180 --> 01:11:22.310
to novelty enough I think.

01:11:22.310 --> 01:11:25.430
And what you just described
cues me to something

01:11:25.430 --> 01:11:28.760
that Dr. Paul Conti and others
have said in terms of trauma.

01:11:28.760 --> 01:11:31.100
And here, I'm paraphrasing,
so my apologies to them

01:11:31.100 --> 01:11:33.060
for not getting
this exactly right,

01:11:33.060 --> 01:11:35.960
that an effective
treatment for trauma

01:11:35.960 --> 01:11:38.210
does not erase the
traumatic memory.

01:11:38.210 --> 01:11:42.200
But it causes a transition
of what once was disturbing,

01:11:42.200 --> 01:11:48.590
and invasive, and maladaptive
to eventually just become

01:11:48.590 --> 01:11:53.300
a boring old story that has
a fuzzy texture to it as

01:11:53.300 --> 01:11:57.290
opposed to this sharp
high friction texture that

01:11:57.290 --> 01:12:00.020
invades our thinking
and obviously,

01:12:00.020 --> 01:12:01.590
our sleeping states as well.

01:12:01.590 --> 01:12:05.570
So again, and I
appreciate the disclaimer,

01:12:05.570 --> 01:12:08.088
the caveats around not being
a clinician, et cetera.

01:12:08.088 --> 01:12:10.130
But I do think that there's
a lot of interest now

01:12:10.130 --> 01:12:12.050
in whether or not
antidepressants are

01:12:12.050 --> 01:12:13.380
effective for trauma or not.

01:12:13.380 --> 01:12:16.370
And I think these aspects
of neuromodulation

01:12:16.370 --> 01:12:20.480
as they relate to, let's call
it, erasing traumas or changing

01:12:20.480 --> 01:12:23.090
the emotional load of
traumas during sleep

01:12:23.090 --> 01:12:24.590
is something important
to take note.

01:12:24.590 --> 01:12:27.132
We also have a lot of clinicians
that listen to this podcast.

01:12:27.132 --> 01:12:29.430
So they should also
take note, please.

01:12:29.430 --> 01:12:34.670
So if I want to reduce the
amount of norepinephrine

01:12:34.670 --> 01:12:38.720
released from locus coeruleus
during rapid eye movement sleep

01:12:38.720 --> 01:12:43.520
to eliminate the troubling or
maybe even traumatic memories

01:12:43.520 --> 01:12:48.350
and allow late stages of
sleep each night to have

01:12:48.350 --> 01:12:51.230
their maximum positive
effect, is there anything

01:12:51.230 --> 01:12:54.260
that I can do besides avoiding--

01:12:54.260 --> 01:12:57.800
avoiding traumas, avoiding
serotonergic or noradrenergic

01:12:57.800 --> 01:12:58.550
compounds?

01:12:58.550 --> 01:13:01.520
GINA POE: Well, I would also
avoid anything just prior

01:13:01.520 --> 01:13:04.680
to going to sleep that
might excite those systems,

01:13:04.680 --> 01:13:10.220
so a lot of novelty, a
lot of the exciting stress

01:13:10.220 --> 01:13:12.710
inducing video games.

01:13:12.710 --> 01:13:18.450
Try and enter sleep with
as much calm as you can,

01:13:18.450 --> 01:13:20.120
so maybe deep
breathing exercises.

01:13:20.120 --> 01:13:24.230
That's a beautiful way to
calm your sympathetic fight

01:13:24.230 --> 01:13:26.120
or flight system
is deep breathing.

01:13:26.120 --> 01:13:29.480
And we haven't been able
to test this with rats

01:13:29.480 --> 01:13:33.680
because we can't ask them to
do a deep breathing exercise.

01:13:33.680 --> 01:13:35.180
There might be a
way we can do that.

01:13:35.180 --> 01:13:38.450
But I haven't found out
or figured it out yet.

01:13:38.450 --> 01:13:42.710
But if there's a way you can
make your sympathetic system,

01:13:42.710 --> 01:13:45.650
nervous system calm down
before you go to sleep,

01:13:45.650 --> 01:13:48.170
might free for you
meditation, or deep breathing

01:13:48.170 --> 01:13:51.020
exercises might be
for some, a warm bath,

01:13:51.020 --> 01:13:55.760
or a comforting book, nothing
too exciting but also nothing

01:13:55.760 --> 01:13:58.640
too boring, perhaps,
just something

01:13:58.640 --> 01:14:02.300
right in the middle which
makes you feel happy and calm

01:14:02.300 --> 01:14:03.480
is what you should do.

01:14:03.480 --> 01:14:06.530
And if you instead go to
sleep while you're anxious

01:14:06.530 --> 01:14:12.500
or you're hyped up, then your
sleep could become maladaptive.

01:14:12.500 --> 01:14:15.890
Another thing that happens
in rats that we have yet

01:14:15.890 --> 01:14:18.680
to know if it
happens in women is

01:14:18.680 --> 01:14:23.330
that female rats have three
phases of their estrus cycle

01:14:23.330 --> 01:14:26.570
that their locus coeruleus
doesn't seem to calm down

01:14:26.570 --> 01:14:27.860
during REM sleep as much.

01:14:27.860 --> 01:14:32.180
And we don't know why, but
during the high estrogen phases

01:14:32.180 --> 01:14:36.110
of their estrus cycle, the
locus coeruleus shuts down

01:14:36.110 --> 01:14:37.460
just like it does in male rats.

01:14:37.460 --> 01:14:39.830
But in the other three
phases, it doesn't.

01:14:39.830 --> 01:14:43.970
So one thing that might
work and in fact, there

01:14:43.970 --> 01:14:46.970
are a few studies that show
it could work really well

01:14:46.970 --> 01:14:53.730
is giving women
after a trauma event,

01:14:53.730 --> 01:14:55.340
something that
contains estrogen,

01:14:55.340 --> 01:14:59.570
because estrogen somehow
is protective against PTSD.

01:14:59.570 --> 01:15:02.360
And they know that through
a retrospective studies,

01:15:02.360 --> 01:15:05.930
where they gave women in
emergency room either a pill

01:15:05.930 --> 01:15:07.730
with estrogen and/or without.

01:15:07.730 --> 01:15:10.040
And those that had the
pill with estrogen in it

01:15:10.040 --> 01:15:12.920
were much less likely to
get PTSD from that trauma

01:15:12.920 --> 01:15:14.660
as measured a year
later than those

01:15:14.660 --> 01:15:15.980
that had the pill without.

01:15:15.980 --> 01:15:22.170
So there's really good
studies by Bronwyn Graham--

01:15:22.170 --> 01:15:24.470
she's out of Australia--
to really hone in

01:15:24.470 --> 01:15:28.130
on how much estrogen do you
need and also testosterone

01:15:28.130 --> 01:15:31.110
just so you know, gets converted
to estrogen in the brain.

01:15:31.110 --> 01:15:33.380
So testosterone also
can be protective,

01:15:33.380 --> 01:15:35.690
because it gets
converted to estrogen.

01:15:35.690 --> 01:15:37.190
But there's something
about estrogen

01:15:37.190 --> 01:15:39.230
that's really helpful
and protective

01:15:39.230 --> 01:15:43.970
about from the high
locus coeruleus firing.

01:15:43.970 --> 01:15:47.270
And this is, again, preliminary
data that we don't have full--

01:15:47.270 --> 01:15:48.800
we don't have all
the answers yet.

01:15:48.800 --> 01:15:52.340
And we are looking into
it actively right now.

01:15:52.340 --> 01:15:53.667
But it's really important.

01:15:53.667 --> 01:15:55.250
The other thing about
women is that we

01:15:55.250 --> 01:15:59.330
are two to four times more
susceptible to anxiety related

01:15:59.330 --> 01:16:01.910
mental health disorders
including post-traumatic stress

01:16:01.910 --> 01:16:02.730
disorder.

01:16:02.730 --> 01:16:06.170
So if we could figure out what's
happening to locus coeruleus

01:16:06.170 --> 01:16:09.920
during sleep in women
and then figure out a way

01:16:09.920 --> 01:16:13.700
to normalize that so the
locus coeruleus is silent

01:16:13.700 --> 01:16:15.260
when it needs to
be silent, I think

01:16:15.260 --> 01:16:17.870
we could go a long
way in helping

01:16:17.870 --> 01:16:22.910
women be more resilient to
stress related disorders.

01:16:22.910 --> 01:16:25.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What are
some other sex differences

01:16:25.220 --> 01:16:27.265
as they relate to sleep?

01:16:27.265 --> 01:16:27.890
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:16:27.890 --> 01:16:29.280
That's a really good question.

01:16:29.280 --> 01:16:34.340
There have been very few
studies, unfortunately,

01:16:34.340 --> 01:16:38.840
of women and sleep, women and
estrus cycle or menstrual cycle

01:16:38.840 --> 01:16:40.700
and sleep.

01:16:40.700 --> 01:16:44.840
But what we have found which
actually largely replicated

01:16:44.840 --> 01:16:51.020
a study in 1960 is
that women or females

01:16:51.020 --> 01:16:54.140
rather at high estrogen,
high hormonal phases

01:16:54.140 --> 01:16:57.380
of their estrus cycle
or menstrual cycle sleep

01:16:57.380 --> 01:16:58.310
a lot less.

01:16:58.310 --> 01:17:00.450
But that sleep is
more efficient.

01:17:00.450 --> 01:17:04.232
So that sleep is more dense
in those sleep spindles, which

01:17:04.232 --> 01:17:05.690
I haven't gone into
what they might

01:17:05.690 --> 01:17:08.660
do except this connection
between the hippocampus

01:17:08.660 --> 01:17:09.380
and cortex.

01:17:09.380 --> 01:17:12.230
But those sleep spindles are
more dense and more coherent

01:17:12.230 --> 01:17:13.970
across the brain areas.

01:17:13.970 --> 01:17:17.240
The theta cycle which
is 5 to 10 Hertz

01:17:17.240 --> 01:17:19.820
in the hippocampus important
for one-year learning

01:17:19.820 --> 01:17:21.470
and also important
during REM sleep

01:17:21.470 --> 01:17:23.960
is also bigger
and juicier during

01:17:23.960 --> 01:17:25.910
the high hormonal phases.

01:17:25.910 --> 01:17:27.530
So even though
there's less sleep,

01:17:27.530 --> 01:17:28.955
it's more efficient and better.

01:17:32.480 --> 01:17:35.810
So all of that efficiency
seems to be reduced

01:17:35.810 --> 01:17:39.440
in those other hormonal phases.

01:17:39.440 --> 01:17:42.890
So even though you might
sleep a little more,

01:17:42.890 --> 01:17:45.620
you might need more
sleep, in fact,

01:17:45.620 --> 01:17:48.050
in order to accomplish
the same thing that you

01:17:48.050 --> 01:17:53.090
can get with that short
very efficient sleep

01:17:53.090 --> 01:17:54.103
of high hormonal phases.

01:17:54.103 --> 01:17:55.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Very interesting.

01:17:55.520 --> 01:17:58.530
I think there is
a growing trend,

01:17:58.530 --> 01:18:03.470
at least among NIH-funded
grants to require

01:18:03.470 --> 01:18:07.190
that as they refer to it in
the grants of biological sex

01:18:07.190 --> 01:18:07.947
as a variable.

01:18:07.947 --> 01:18:09.530
And here, we're
talking-- nothing here

01:18:09.530 --> 01:18:11.505
about sex, the
verb, although, I'm

01:18:11.505 --> 01:18:13.130
sure there are studies
about that, too.

01:18:13.130 --> 01:18:14.570
But biological
sex is a variable,

01:18:14.570 --> 01:18:17.330
because there is a dearth
of studies exploring sex

01:18:17.330 --> 01:18:20.683
differences in most everything.

01:18:20.683 --> 01:18:22.350
There are all sorts
of reasons for that.

01:18:22.350 --> 01:18:24.500
But more importantly
that fortunately,

01:18:24.500 --> 01:18:25.860
the trend is shifting.

01:18:25.860 --> 01:18:26.930
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:18:26.930 --> 01:18:30.830
And even when you study males
versus females, a lot of people

01:18:30.830 --> 01:18:33.860
just say include
females in their studies

01:18:33.860 --> 01:18:37.520
but then don't track the estrus
cycle or menstrual cycle.

01:18:37.520 --> 01:18:41.600
And hormones have huge
effects on our behavior.

01:18:41.600 --> 01:18:45.828
Just think about-- when you said
sex before our hormones come

01:18:45.828 --> 01:18:47.120
in, we're not interested in it.

01:18:47.120 --> 01:18:50.990
And suddenly, that's kind of
a main driver of behaviors.

01:18:50.990 --> 01:18:54.660
Hormones can definitely change
who we are and what we do.

01:18:54.660 --> 01:18:57.872
So we should be studying
hormones not just sex.

01:18:57.872 --> 01:19:00.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I always
say that puberty is perhaps

01:19:00.080 --> 01:19:02.443
the most massive
transformation and rate

01:19:02.443 --> 01:19:04.610
of aging that any of us go
through in a short amount

01:19:04.610 --> 01:19:05.150
of time.

01:19:05.150 --> 01:19:06.910
An individual, their
cognition changes.

01:19:06.910 --> 01:19:07.910
Their worldview changes.

01:19:07.910 --> 01:19:09.830
And that's largely
hormonal-driven.

01:19:09.830 --> 01:19:13.100
And obviously, neural
architectures change, too.

01:19:13.100 --> 01:19:15.890
I'm very happy that
you mentioned--

01:19:15.890 --> 01:19:17.630
I'm trying to get
into calmer states

01:19:17.630 --> 01:19:20.580
prior to sleep and
some ways to do that.

01:19:20.580 --> 01:19:23.000
I'm a big fan and I've talked
a lot before on this podcast

01:19:23.000 --> 01:19:26.600
about things like yoga nidra
which is a non-movement based

01:19:26.600 --> 01:19:29.330
practice, sometimes called
non-sleep depressed where

01:19:29.330 --> 01:19:31.970
people actually take some
time each day to practice how

01:19:31.970 --> 01:19:34.550
to go into a more
parasympathetic a.k.a relaxed

01:19:34.550 --> 01:19:37.015
state deliberately, because
it's a bit of a skill.

01:19:37.015 --> 01:19:37.640
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:19:37.640 --> 01:19:39.973
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's-- and
there there's some good data

01:19:39.973 --> 01:19:42.170
really, mostly out of a
laboratory in Scandinavia

01:19:42.170 --> 01:19:47.600
showing huge increases in
nigrostriatal dopamine when

01:19:47.600 --> 01:19:49.100
people go--

01:19:49.100 --> 01:19:53.690
basically, engage in a practice
of deliberate non-movement

01:19:53.690 --> 01:19:55.130
and that the brain
actually enters

01:19:55.130 --> 01:19:56.870
states of a very shallow sleep.

01:19:56.870 --> 01:19:59.510
So it's sort of nap-ish.

01:19:59.510 --> 01:20:01.940
But the idea is to actually
stay awake but motionless.

01:20:01.940 --> 01:20:04.610
And it does seem to restore
a certain number of features

01:20:04.610 --> 01:20:05.360
of neurochemistry.

01:20:05.360 --> 01:20:07.700
But perhaps more
importantly, it teaches

01:20:07.700 --> 01:20:11.000
people to relax which is
something that most people are

01:20:11.000 --> 01:20:12.680
not very good at.

01:20:12.680 --> 01:20:15.038
But in any event--

01:20:15.038 --> 01:20:16.580
and people who listen
to this podcast

01:20:16.580 --> 01:20:18.120
have heard me say this
over and over again,

01:20:18.120 --> 01:20:20.203
so I sound like a broken
record, but this practice

01:20:20.203 --> 01:20:22.190
as a zero cost practice
that doesn't require

01:20:22.190 --> 01:20:26.000
any pharmacology does seem to
really enhance people's ability

01:20:26.000 --> 01:20:28.447
to fall asleep more
quickly and to fall back

01:20:28.447 --> 01:20:30.530
asleep if they wake up in
the middle of the night.

01:20:30.530 --> 01:20:33.680
So in any event, another
plug for NSDR, yoga nidra.

01:20:33.680 --> 01:20:36.230
GINA POE: Well, I
just also want to add

01:20:36.230 --> 01:20:38.360
to that, that's one of
the reasons why insomnia

01:20:38.360 --> 01:20:41.240
is so insidious is because when
people feel like they haven't

01:20:41.240 --> 01:20:42.950
gotten enough sleep, and they're
not getting enough sleep,

01:20:42.950 --> 01:20:44.630
and they become anxious
about getting enough sleep,

01:20:44.630 --> 01:20:46.588
and then they're anxious
before going to sleep,

01:20:46.588 --> 01:20:48.005
like I'm not going
to fall asleep,

01:20:48.005 --> 01:20:49.880
it's going to be 45
minutes, and then that's

01:20:49.880 --> 01:20:51.450
a positive feedback loop.

01:20:51.450 --> 01:20:54.230
So you need to break
that loop, say, OK,

01:20:54.230 --> 01:20:56.330
my body is going to get
as much sleep as it needs,

01:20:56.330 --> 01:21:00.470
I needn't worry about it, and
then practice this relaxation

01:21:00.470 --> 01:21:04.940
to say, hey it's all OK,
it's going to be all right,

01:21:04.940 --> 01:21:08.360
and then concentrate on
things that relax you,

01:21:08.360 --> 01:21:09.890
whether it's
concentrating or not

01:21:09.890 --> 01:21:12.350
concentrating, whatever it is.

01:21:12.350 --> 01:21:15.590
You mentioned yoga nidra.

01:21:15.590 --> 01:21:20.760
And that reminded me of
transcendental meditation

01:21:20.760 --> 01:21:23.420
which is something that also
hasn't been studied well,

01:21:23.420 --> 01:21:26.760
largely because we can't ask
nonhuman animals to do it.

01:21:26.760 --> 01:21:29.000
And so we don't know
what's happening

01:21:29.000 --> 01:21:33.710
with our neurochemistry
and our brain activity

01:21:33.710 --> 01:21:36.450
in a deep and meaningful way.

01:21:36.450 --> 01:21:38.750
But one thing that
has been shown

01:21:38.750 --> 01:21:40.250
in those that can
do it really well

01:21:40.250 --> 01:21:43.040
is that, that theta
activity that I said

01:21:43.040 --> 01:21:44.870
happens when you're
learning something

01:21:44.870 --> 01:21:49.250
or when you're in REM
sleep, it's well established

01:21:49.250 --> 01:21:53.540
and increases during the
transcendental meditation.

01:21:53.540 --> 01:21:57.290
So it might be that some
states of meditation

01:21:57.290 --> 01:22:01.280
could in some ways
replace or mimic

01:22:01.280 --> 01:22:03.710
some functions of, for
example, REM sleep.

01:22:03.710 --> 01:22:06.573
But again, we don't know
if all the neurochemistry

01:22:06.573 --> 01:22:09.240
is right to do, for example, the
thing that I was talking about,

01:22:09.240 --> 01:22:11.928
which is erasing the
novelty encoding structures

01:22:11.928 --> 01:22:12.470
of the brain.

01:22:12.470 --> 01:22:15.960
That needs an absence of
norepinephrine and serotonin,

01:22:15.960 --> 01:22:18.380
which we don't know
if that goes away

01:22:18.380 --> 01:22:20.300
with transcendental meditation.

01:22:20.300 --> 01:22:22.490
We just don't know the
answer to that yet.

01:22:22.490 --> 01:22:23.407
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:22:23.407 --> 01:22:25.910
The studies on yoga nidra
and sleep replacement

01:22:25.910 --> 01:22:27.590
are interesting.

01:22:27.590 --> 01:22:30.710
It does seem to be the case
that nothing can really

01:22:30.710 --> 01:22:32.310
replace sleep except sleep.

01:22:32.310 --> 01:22:34.083
But that if one
is sleep deprived

01:22:34.083 --> 01:22:35.750
or is having trouble
falling back asleep

01:22:35.750 --> 01:22:39.110
that these things like--

01:22:39.110 --> 01:22:40.190
and I hear it's--

01:22:40.190 --> 01:22:42.590
I acknowledge this is
essentially like yoga nidra,

01:22:42.590 --> 01:22:45.140
but we now call it
non-sleep deep rest or NSDR,

01:22:45.140 --> 01:22:47.750
because oftentimes, for
names like yoga nidra

01:22:47.750 --> 01:22:51.590
act as a barrier for what
would otherwise be people

01:22:51.590 --> 01:22:52.700
willing to try a practice.

01:22:52.700 --> 01:22:53.870
It sounds mystical.

01:22:53.870 --> 01:22:54.350
GINA POE: --and yoga.

01:22:54.350 --> 01:22:56.308
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It sounds
like flying carpets.

01:22:56.308 --> 01:22:58.210
And it sounds like you
have to go to Esalen.

01:22:58.210 --> 01:22:59.877
By the way, Esalen
is a beautiful place.

01:22:59.877 --> 01:23:02.335
But it sounds like you have to
go there or live in the West

01:23:02.335 --> 01:23:03.760
Coast to believe in this stuff.

01:23:03.760 --> 01:23:05.030
But it's simply not the case.

01:23:05.030 --> 01:23:07.210
These are practices
that are really

01:23:07.210 --> 01:23:12.160
just self-directed relaxation
as a practice that allows people

01:23:12.160 --> 01:23:14.890
to get better and better
at directing their brain

01:23:14.890 --> 01:23:16.840
states towards more relaxation.

01:23:16.840 --> 01:23:18.460
And most people
have an asymmetry.

01:23:18.460 --> 01:23:20.230
Like for instance,
most people can force

01:23:20.230 --> 01:23:22.340
themselves to stay up later.

01:23:22.340 --> 01:23:24.730
But they have a hard time
going to sleep earlier.

01:23:24.730 --> 01:23:26.440
And that just speaks
to the asymmetry

01:23:26.440 --> 01:23:28.930
that's probably adaptive
in survival-based

01:23:28.930 --> 01:23:32.050
that we can ramp ourselves up
far more easily than we can

01:23:32.050 --> 01:23:33.280
tend to calm ourselves down.

01:23:33.280 --> 01:23:33.905
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:23:33.905 --> 01:23:37.990
And actually to appeal to
other Christians like me,

01:23:37.990 --> 01:23:41.680
prayer can be a wonderful
way to calm yourself down,

01:23:41.680 --> 01:23:46.150
because through prayer you're
giving your carries to God

01:23:46.150 --> 01:23:47.650
and saying--

01:23:47.650 --> 01:23:51.200
and then you are
relaxed, more relaxed.

01:23:51.200 --> 01:23:54.400
And I just want to say that
because, the same reason

01:23:54.400 --> 01:23:56.050
that yoga might put
some people off.

01:23:56.050 --> 01:24:00.190
It might put some people
off to talk about prayer,

01:24:00.190 --> 01:24:07.165
but it's the same process of
being able to relax, and yeah.

01:24:07.165 --> 01:24:09.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And get
outside our own experience

01:24:09.290 --> 01:24:09.940
a little bit.

01:24:09.940 --> 01:24:11.020
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:24:11.020 --> 01:24:13.570
Back out get a world
view that might actually

01:24:13.570 --> 01:24:15.845
also help us to relax.

01:24:15.845 --> 01:24:17.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
you might be surprised

01:24:17.720 --> 01:24:19.720
at how many clinicians
and scientists who've

01:24:19.720 --> 01:24:22.990
come on this podcast have
mentioned things like prayer

01:24:22.990 --> 01:24:25.570
from various perspectives,
Christianity, Judaism,

01:24:25.570 --> 01:24:28.555
Muslim traditions,
and others that--

01:24:28.555 --> 01:24:30.380
as a parallel to
all of these things.

01:24:30.380 --> 01:24:33.640
And I think what it speaks to
is the fact that, ultimately,

01:24:33.640 --> 01:24:36.550
the biological architectures
that we're all contending with

01:24:36.550 --> 01:24:38.050
are going to be
identical, right?

01:24:38.050 --> 01:24:40.210
And so different ways to
tap into them and ones

01:24:40.210 --> 01:24:45.640
that are congruent with people's
beliefs, I think are great.

01:24:45.640 --> 01:24:47.020
GINA POE: Yeah,
because anything,

01:24:47.020 --> 01:24:49.103
non-congruent with your
beliefs is also stressful.

01:24:49.103 --> 01:24:50.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And feels forced.

01:24:50.520 --> 01:24:52.510
And that's why this
idea of calling it

01:24:52.510 --> 01:24:54.700
non-sleep deep rest in
addition to yoga nidra

01:24:54.700 --> 01:24:57.250
was not to detract from
the naming or the history

01:24:57.250 --> 01:24:58.300
around yoga nidra.

01:24:58.300 --> 01:25:00.830
But I was finding
that it was a barrier.

01:25:00.830 --> 01:25:04.420
Likewise, yoga nidra tends to
include things like intentions

01:25:04.420 --> 01:25:05.580
whereas NSDR scripts.

01:25:05.580 --> 01:25:08.080
And by the way, we will provide
links to some NSDRs and yoga

01:25:08.080 --> 01:25:08.710
nidra scripts.

01:25:08.710 --> 01:25:10.960
But NSDR has no intentions.

01:25:10.960 --> 01:25:13.510
It's simply a body scan
deep relaxation-based.

01:25:13.510 --> 01:25:16.090
So it's the scientific
version of all of this stuff.

01:25:16.090 --> 01:25:18.325
And actually, we study
it in the laboratory

01:25:18.325 --> 01:25:20.410
and some of the brain
states that people go into.

01:25:20.410 --> 01:25:22.718
But that's a discussion
for another time.

01:25:22.718 --> 01:25:24.010
GINA POE: This is hard not to--

01:25:24.010 --> 01:25:26.410
My mother used to tell
me when I would complain,

01:25:26.410 --> 01:25:30.040
I can't go to sleep, she'd
say, well, start with your toes

01:25:30.040 --> 01:25:30.940
and relax.

01:25:30.940 --> 01:25:33.850
So you would clench your
muscles around your toes

01:25:33.850 --> 01:25:36.588
then relax them and do that
all the way from your toes

01:25:36.588 --> 01:25:37.630
all the way to your head.

01:25:37.630 --> 01:25:38.950
And I don't know
where she got this,

01:25:38.950 --> 01:25:40.600
it might have been
her own common sense

01:25:40.600 --> 01:25:44.740
or she might have gotten
it from this NPR shows

01:25:44.740 --> 01:25:47.980
called The Mind Can Keep You
Well she used to listen to.

01:25:47.980 --> 01:25:50.680
But that's another
intentional relaxation

01:25:50.680 --> 01:25:54.040
that focuses on the
body rather than

01:25:54.040 --> 01:25:56.538
on your own mental
processes, but.

01:25:56.538 --> 01:25:59.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I do a little
bit of work with the military.

01:25:59.080 --> 01:26:01.990
And there's a method
within certain communities

01:26:01.990 --> 01:26:03.880
of Special Operations
in the US military,

01:26:03.880 --> 01:26:06.790
where if they can't sleep
or they're having challenges

01:26:06.790 --> 01:26:10.150
sleeping, they will
deliberately try and relax

01:26:10.150 --> 01:26:13.120
their facial muscles in
particular like drape

01:26:13.120 --> 01:26:17.140
the facial muscles
and use long or exhale

01:26:17.140 --> 01:26:20.620
emphasized breathing
does seem to increase

01:26:20.620 --> 01:26:23.080
the probability of
transitioning back into sleep.

01:26:23.080 --> 01:26:27.190
And those are hallmarks of yoga
nidra, non-sleep deep rest,

01:26:27.190 --> 01:26:28.293
body scans.

01:26:28.293 --> 01:26:29.710
And so I think all
of these things

01:26:29.710 --> 01:26:31.600
converge on a common theme.

01:26:31.600 --> 01:26:34.420
As neurobiologist, we can
say, all of the things

01:26:34.420 --> 01:26:38.200
that we are describing
certainly move the needle away

01:26:38.200 --> 01:26:39.880
from locus coeruleus activation.

01:26:39.880 --> 01:26:41.440
And we haven't
done the experiment

01:26:41.440 --> 01:26:42.398
to really look at that.

01:26:42.398 --> 01:26:45.380
But it seems all these things
are counter to noradrenaline

01:26:45.380 --> 01:26:45.880
release.

01:26:45.880 --> 01:26:46.547
GINA POE: Right.

01:26:46.547 --> 01:26:48.040
Another one is yawning.

01:26:48.040 --> 01:26:50.320
Yawning in itself
is, in fact, kind

01:26:50.320 --> 01:26:53.740
of tensing of all the
muscles in your face

01:26:53.740 --> 01:26:55.100
and then relaxing them.

01:26:55.100 --> 01:26:56.920
So it might be why we yawn.

01:26:56.920 --> 01:26:58.210
We don't know why we yawn yet.

01:26:58.210 --> 01:27:01.270
But it might also-- it
would be really great.

01:27:01.270 --> 01:27:03.240
Actually, animals yawn, too.

01:27:03.240 --> 01:27:05.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN: My bulldog
was a-- perpetually.

01:27:05.240 --> 01:27:07.565
If he wasn't sleeping,
he was yawning.

01:27:07.565 --> 01:27:09.940
GINA POE: And it would be
interesting to see what yawning

01:27:09.940 --> 01:27:11.107
does to the locus coeruleus.

01:27:11.107 --> 01:27:14.080
Does that also calm and switch
the locus coeruleus activity,

01:27:14.080 --> 01:27:17.350
because it's an interesting
that facial nerve

01:27:17.350 --> 01:27:20.380
like trigeminal nerve
through the vagus

01:27:20.380 --> 01:27:23.860
connects indirectly
to the locus coeruleus

01:27:23.860 --> 01:27:25.630
and has a powerful
effect on that.

01:27:25.630 --> 01:27:26.838
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

01:27:26.838 --> 01:27:29.470
A common, I think friend of ours
and direct colleague of yours,

01:27:29.470 --> 01:27:32.107
Jack Feldman was a guest
on this podcast telling us

01:27:32.107 --> 01:27:34.690
about all the amazing structures
he and others have discovered

01:27:34.690 --> 01:27:36.617
in respiration and breathing.

01:27:36.617 --> 01:27:38.200
It sounds like we
have a collaboration

01:27:38.200 --> 01:27:41.925
brewing the three of us
should definitely carry out.

01:27:41.925 --> 01:27:44.050
I'd love for you to share
with us a little bit more

01:27:44.050 --> 01:27:47.230
about the spindles that
have come up a few times.

01:27:47.230 --> 01:27:49.250
And I don't know if
it's relevant to this.

01:27:49.250 --> 01:27:51.520
So if it's not, let's
separate it out.

01:27:51.520 --> 01:27:53.830
But I'd love for you
to tell us a little bit

01:27:53.830 --> 01:27:57.625
about the role of sleep in
problem solving and creativity.

01:27:57.625 --> 01:27:59.250
And if spindles are
involved, then I'll

01:27:59.250 --> 01:28:00.995
consider myself lucky
for batching them

01:28:00.995 --> 01:28:01.870
in the same question.

01:28:01.870 --> 01:28:03.240
And if they're not
involved, simply

01:28:03.240 --> 01:28:04.572
feel free to separate them out.

01:28:04.572 --> 01:28:06.280
GINA POE: I think they
could be involved.

01:28:06.280 --> 01:28:07.590
And the reason why
I think they could

01:28:07.590 --> 01:28:10.007
be involved because we now
know a lot more about spindles.

01:28:10.007 --> 01:28:11.880
First of all, the first
thing that we knew--

01:28:11.880 --> 01:28:13.350
first of all, we ignored them.

01:28:13.350 --> 01:28:14.880
Then we thought
they had something

01:28:14.880 --> 01:28:16.110
to do with keeping us asleep.

01:28:16.110 --> 01:28:18.965
And that was their function is
when an external stimulus came,

01:28:18.965 --> 01:28:21.090
they would keep us asleep
because they would arise.

01:28:21.090 --> 01:28:23.790
But now, we know that
the density of our sleep

01:28:23.790 --> 01:28:26.430
spindles, the number that
we produce per minute

01:28:26.430 --> 01:28:28.860
is well correlated
with our intelligence

01:28:28.860 --> 01:28:30.480
in the first place
and that no matter

01:28:30.480 --> 01:28:33.840
what your intelligence is and no
matter what your sleep spindle

01:28:33.840 --> 01:28:36.840
density is, if you're learning
something during the day

01:28:36.840 --> 01:28:38.970
and increase your
sleep spindle density,

01:28:38.970 --> 01:28:42.420
it's really almost perfectly
correlated with our ability

01:28:42.420 --> 01:28:45.510
to consolidate that
information and incorporate it

01:28:45.510 --> 01:28:47.980
into the schema that we
already have in our brain.

01:28:47.980 --> 01:28:50.560
So if you try and
learn something new,

01:28:50.560 --> 01:28:52.500
even if your sleep spindle
density at baseline

01:28:52.500 --> 01:28:55.860
is great, if you don't increase
your sleep spindles that night,

01:28:55.860 --> 01:28:59.610
you're not going to use
sleep to really incorporate.

01:28:59.610 --> 01:29:02.250
Interestingly,
sleep spindles are

01:29:02.250 --> 01:29:04.290
poor in those with
schizophrenia.

01:29:04.290 --> 01:29:07.230
It's one of the
characteristic signatures

01:29:07.230 --> 01:29:10.500
of sleep is that sleep spindles
are very few and far between,

01:29:10.500 --> 01:29:14.880
which might mean that
people with schizophrenia

01:29:14.880 --> 01:29:18.300
might not be able to incorporate
new information into already

01:29:18.300 --> 01:29:19.260
existing schema.

01:29:19.260 --> 01:29:22.470
And instead, it flaps
in the breeze out there

01:29:22.470 --> 01:29:26.340
and can be accessed
erroneously at times when you

01:29:26.340 --> 01:29:28.810
don't want it to be involved.

01:29:28.810 --> 01:29:32.350
So I digress, so sleep
spindles and creativity.

01:29:32.350 --> 01:29:35.220
So one of the things we now
through some great studies

01:29:35.220 --> 01:29:39.390
by Julie Seibt and Anita
Lüthi is that sleep spindles

01:29:39.390 --> 01:29:43.320
are accompanied by an
incredible plasticity

01:29:43.320 --> 01:29:45.990
out in the distal
dendrites, the listening

01:29:45.990 --> 01:29:50.890
branches of our neurons that
listen to other cortical areas.

01:29:50.890 --> 01:29:53.340
So there are proximal
dendrites in our neurons

01:29:53.340 --> 01:29:56.070
that listen to
the external world

01:29:56.070 --> 01:29:58.200
and are conducted
through the thalamus.

01:29:58.200 --> 01:29:59.830
And then there are
distal dendrites

01:29:59.830 --> 01:30:03.810
which listen to an internal
conversation that's

01:30:03.810 --> 01:30:05.250
happening in our brains.

01:30:05.250 --> 01:30:08.340
It's kind of our
internal state, really.

01:30:08.340 --> 01:30:10.050
And during sleep
spindles, that's

01:30:10.050 --> 01:30:14.610
when those distal dendrites
are able to best learn

01:30:14.610 --> 01:30:18.240
from other cortical areas
and from the hippocampus.

01:30:18.240 --> 01:30:20.940
It is during sleep spindles that
the hippocampus and the cortex

01:30:20.940 --> 01:30:25.110
are best connected and when
that incredible plasticity

01:30:25.110 --> 01:30:25.890
can happen.

01:30:25.890 --> 01:30:28.960
When I talk about schema, that's
a cortical, cortical thing.

01:30:28.960 --> 01:30:32.520
That's when the image of
Santa Claus and presents

01:30:32.520 --> 01:30:33.330
comes together.

01:30:33.330 --> 01:30:35.010
It's not through
some external thing.

01:30:35.010 --> 01:30:37.060
Once we learn those
things together,

01:30:37.060 --> 01:30:39.810
it's our cortex that
encodes that and brings

01:30:39.810 --> 01:30:41.850
those images back up together.

01:30:41.850 --> 01:30:43.950
And that's during
sleep spindles when

01:30:43.950 --> 01:30:45.870
that's happening, when that--

01:30:45.870 --> 01:30:51.060
there's big surges of calcium
into those distal dendrites,

01:30:51.060 --> 01:30:55.560
and where plasticity happens
in just huge amounts.

01:30:55.560 --> 01:30:59.550
During that sleep spindle stage
of sleep which is N2 stage,

01:30:59.550 --> 01:31:03.062
there's also another
excitatory event

01:31:03.062 --> 01:31:04.770
that comes all the
way from the brainstem

01:31:04.770 --> 01:31:07.530
and projects everywhere
in our cortex which

01:31:07.530 --> 01:31:09.360
is called PGO waves.

01:31:09.360 --> 01:31:12.967
That's P for pons, G
for geniculo nucleus

01:31:12.967 --> 01:31:15.300
of the thalamus which is where
they're first discovered,

01:31:15.300 --> 01:31:17.670
and O for occipital
area which is

01:31:17.670 --> 01:31:20.550
our visual area which is, again,
where they're first discovered.

01:31:20.550 --> 01:31:24.270
But in fact, it's now been shown
that PGO waves, which we should

01:31:24.270 --> 01:31:26.700
generalize to P waves because
they come from the pons

01:31:26.700 --> 01:31:29.100
and go to the thalamus
and then the cortex,

01:31:29.100 --> 01:31:30.600
happens all over the brains.

01:31:30.600 --> 01:31:34.830
And that is where glutamate,
which is a major excitatory

01:31:34.830 --> 01:31:37.800
neurotransmitter involved
in learning and plasticity

01:31:37.800 --> 01:31:39.960
is being released
in big amounts,

01:31:39.960 --> 01:31:41.650
also in those distal dendrites.

01:31:41.650 --> 01:31:43.890
So P waves and
spindles work together

01:31:43.890 --> 01:31:47.140
to cause plasticity and
sew our schema together

01:31:47.140 --> 01:31:51.120
which could be the origins
for insight and creativity.

01:31:51.120 --> 01:31:54.570
Now, when PGO waves or P
waves were first discovered,

01:31:54.570 --> 01:31:59.010
it was thought to be random,
because this small area that

01:31:59.010 --> 01:32:02.640
generates P waves
all over the brain

01:32:02.640 --> 01:32:06.510
projects all over the thalamus
and causes P waves all over.

01:32:06.510 --> 01:32:09.720
And you don't measure P
waves all over the brain

01:32:09.720 --> 01:32:10.570
at the same time.

01:32:10.570 --> 01:32:13.330
In fact, it's just seems
sporadic and random.

01:32:13.330 --> 01:32:17.310
So that's probably-- and P
waves also happening even more

01:32:17.310 --> 01:32:19.410
during REM sleep, rapid
eye movement sleep.

01:32:19.410 --> 01:32:25.020
So that's why people
think that REM dreams are

01:32:25.020 --> 01:32:27.780
so random is because
these P waves are random.

01:32:27.780 --> 01:32:30.840
And they could generate
dreams because they're

01:32:30.840 --> 01:32:37.170
an internal source of excitation
that replaces the outside world

01:32:37.170 --> 01:32:38.610
during our dream state.

01:32:38.610 --> 01:32:43.830
And so these P waves, if they
are random, could function--

01:32:43.830 --> 01:32:45.750
could be the
underlying reason why

01:32:45.750 --> 01:32:48.180
REM sleep dreams are random.

01:32:48.180 --> 01:32:52.260
And it might also be why
creativity can happen there

01:32:52.260 --> 01:32:54.570
because we're
randomly activating--

01:32:54.570 --> 01:32:56.820
co-activating different
things in our brain

01:32:56.820 --> 01:32:59.040
that we can then sew together.

01:32:59.040 --> 01:33:01.110
But it might not be
as random as we think.

01:33:01.110 --> 01:33:02.512
So that's the caveat there.

01:33:02.512 --> 01:33:04.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I just
learned a lot from you,

01:33:04.470 --> 01:33:07.960
because I teach brain
stem to medical students.

01:33:07.960 --> 01:33:10.160
And I talk about the pons.

01:33:10.160 --> 01:33:11.910
And the pons is like
this dense collection

01:33:11.910 --> 01:33:13.680
of all these different
nuclei involved

01:33:13.680 --> 01:33:15.070
in a bunch of different things.

01:33:15.070 --> 01:33:17.520
And it's close by a bunch
of interesting things.

01:33:17.520 --> 01:33:20.460
And it's still a
mysterious brain area.

01:33:20.460 --> 01:33:22.500
But when I learned
about PGO waves,

01:33:22.500 --> 01:33:24.620
I thought
pons-geniculo-occipital,

01:33:24.620 --> 01:33:26.760
because occipital is
most commonly associated

01:33:26.760 --> 01:33:29.430
with visual cortex,
I thought it was

01:33:29.430 --> 01:33:32.475
the origin of the visual
component of dreams.

01:33:32.475 --> 01:33:33.750
GINA POE: And probably is.

01:33:33.750 --> 01:33:35.417
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm
very happy to learn

01:33:35.417 --> 01:33:38.178
that they should be called P
waves, because they include

01:33:38.178 --> 01:33:39.720
lots of different
areas of the brain.

01:33:39.720 --> 01:33:42.060
And it makes really
good sense to me

01:33:42.060 --> 01:33:45.930
why the pseudo
randomness of dreams

01:33:45.930 --> 01:33:49.170
especially these late
night and early morning--

01:33:49.170 --> 01:33:52.290
later in sleep I should say
and early morning dreams

01:33:52.290 --> 01:33:57.285
seem to be cobbled together
from disparate experiences.

01:33:57.285 --> 01:33:59.190
You walk through a
door and suddenly, it's

01:33:59.190 --> 01:34:01.300
a completely different
context and landscape.

01:34:01.300 --> 01:34:03.013
GINA POE: Yes,
beautiful. beautiful.

01:34:03.013 --> 01:34:04.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
like this idea a lot.

01:34:04.680 --> 01:34:07.133
It makes intuitive sense
it makes biological sense.

01:34:07.133 --> 01:34:08.550
It also gives me
something to talk

01:34:08.550 --> 01:34:10.300
about to the medical
students next quarter

01:34:10.300 --> 01:34:11.310
when I talk about pons.

01:34:11.310 --> 01:34:11.430
GINA POE: Right.

01:34:11.430 --> 01:34:13.138
You want to talk about
where in the pons,

01:34:13.138 --> 01:34:14.790
it's right below
the locus coeruleus.

01:34:14.790 --> 01:34:16.320
It's called the sub coeruleus.

01:34:16.320 --> 01:34:17.670
They're glutamatergic.

01:34:17.670 --> 01:34:23.940
It's also called SLD, sub
lateral dorsal nucleus, so.

01:34:23.940 --> 01:34:26.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So note to
any aspiring neurobiologist,

01:34:26.970 --> 01:34:31.167
there's a vast landscape of yet
to be undiscovered structure

01:34:31.167 --> 01:34:32.250
and functions in the pons.

01:34:32.250 --> 01:34:33.750
You want to work
on something that

01:34:33.750 --> 01:34:36.180
is sure to reveal something
novel, work on the pons

01:34:36.180 --> 01:34:37.630
because it's in every textbook.

01:34:37.630 --> 01:34:41.040
It's clinically very
important structure.

01:34:41.040 --> 01:34:46.680
Sadly, gliomas and other cancers
of the brain can sometimes--

01:34:46.680 --> 01:34:48.720
can often surface in the pons.

01:34:48.720 --> 01:34:52.170
But we still know
very little about it.

01:34:52.170 --> 01:34:54.930
I read a paper this
last year or-- and I

01:34:54.930 --> 01:34:57.090
think it was covered in
a bit of popular press,

01:34:57.090 --> 01:35:00.360
that during rapid
eye movement sleep,

01:35:00.360 --> 01:35:04.708
people can solve problems or
respond to external stimuli.

01:35:04.708 --> 01:35:06.750
For instance, they would
give them math problems.

01:35:06.750 --> 01:35:08.542
They'd whisper in their
ear while they were

01:35:08.542 --> 01:35:09.992
in REM sleep, what's 2 plus 2.

01:35:09.992 --> 01:35:12.450
And then people would say, even
though they were paralyzed,

01:35:12.450 --> 01:35:14.325
apparently they could
still move their mouth,

01:35:14.325 --> 01:35:16.860
because they'd say four
or something like that.

01:35:16.860 --> 01:35:18.498
Or they'd say, what's your name.

01:35:18.498 --> 01:35:19.540
And people could respond.

01:35:19.540 --> 01:35:22.140
And so that in REM
sleep, perhaps, people--

01:35:22.140 --> 01:35:25.470
some elements of cognition
are still active.

01:35:25.470 --> 01:35:27.480
GINA POE: I'm glad
you brought that up.

01:35:27.480 --> 01:35:29.147
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
are you thinking--

01:35:29.147 --> 01:35:31.650
and I don't know the
authors of that study and--

01:35:31.650 --> 01:35:33.660
listen, if ever I
say something wrong,

01:35:33.660 --> 01:35:36.300
it's great on this podcast
because someone will tell us

01:35:36.300 --> 01:35:37.380
in the YouTube comments.

01:35:37.380 --> 01:35:39.750
It's one of the great
uses of YouTube comments.

01:35:39.750 --> 01:35:42.240
But I'd love to know your
thoughts on that study.

01:35:42.240 --> 01:35:47.490
Is that just kind of an
odd feature that or does

01:35:47.490 --> 01:35:48.240
this have meaning.

01:35:48.240 --> 01:35:49.830
Should we actually
care about this result?

01:35:49.830 --> 01:35:51.330
GINA POE: There is
no just about it.

01:35:51.330 --> 01:35:53.910
It's really actually
intriguing and interesting

01:35:53.910 --> 01:35:55.890
and might relate to
this paper that I

01:35:55.890 --> 01:35:58.440
talked about where we said
different areas of the brain

01:35:58.440 --> 01:36:00.670
can be in different
states at the same time.

01:36:00.670 --> 01:36:03.000
So lucid dreaming
is another thing

01:36:03.000 --> 01:36:05.400
we can't ask animals
to do or can't ask them

01:36:05.400 --> 01:36:06.360
if they've done it.

01:36:06.360 --> 01:36:09.600
But we can certainly
ask humans to do it.

01:36:09.600 --> 01:36:11.490
And some people can
do it really well.

01:36:11.490 --> 01:36:13.620
And it would be really
interesting to see

01:36:13.620 --> 01:36:16.740
in those people who could lucid
dream really well whether they

01:36:16.740 --> 01:36:21.300
spend more or less time
in this asymmetrical state

01:36:21.300 --> 01:36:23.097
where one area of the
brain is in one state

01:36:23.097 --> 01:36:24.930
and another area of the
brain is in another.

01:36:24.930 --> 01:36:28.740
And it might be that those
people can respond to questions

01:36:28.740 --> 01:36:29.790
during REM sleep.

01:36:29.790 --> 01:36:35.100
Best are those that have the
most asymmetry or dissimilarity

01:36:35.100 --> 01:36:37.890
or dissociation between
subcortical and cortical

01:36:37.890 --> 01:36:38.490
structures.

01:36:38.490 --> 01:36:40.990
Or it might be that they're the
ones with the most symmetry,

01:36:40.990 --> 01:36:42.690
we don't know.

01:36:42.690 --> 01:36:45.240
I do worry a little bit
about lucid dreaming,

01:36:45.240 --> 01:36:46.590
because people are-- it's a fad.

01:36:46.590 --> 01:36:48.048
People are really
excited about it.

01:36:48.048 --> 01:36:49.770
And to be able to
remember one's dreams

01:36:49.770 --> 01:36:52.983
is fun often unless
there are nightmares.

01:36:52.983 --> 01:36:54.150
But it's really interesting.

01:36:54.150 --> 01:36:56.670
Or to be able to direct one's
dreams if they are a nightmare

01:36:56.670 --> 01:36:59.280
is really a wonderful
power to have,

01:36:59.280 --> 01:37:01.830
to be able to redirect
a nightmare that

01:37:01.830 --> 01:37:03.480
has been repeated
to something else

01:37:03.480 --> 01:37:05.580
and then kick
yourself out of that.

01:37:05.580 --> 01:37:07.800
Repetitive nightmare
is really nice.

01:37:07.800 --> 01:37:10.110
But I worry a
little bit about it,

01:37:10.110 --> 01:37:13.140
because we know so
little about what's

01:37:13.140 --> 01:37:14.590
actually going on in the brain.

01:37:14.590 --> 01:37:18.600
And if this lucid dreaming
state is preventing us

01:37:18.600 --> 01:37:21.150
from, for example, from the
locus coeruleus from calming

01:37:21.150 --> 01:37:25.650
down or the serotonergic system
from silencing like it should,

01:37:25.650 --> 01:37:27.960
and maybe what we're
doing during this state

01:37:27.960 --> 01:37:30.750
is Yeah we're activating the
learning and memory structures

01:37:30.750 --> 01:37:34.800
but in a way that's maladaptive
in terms of the erasure

01:37:34.800 --> 01:37:35.730
that we need to do.

01:37:35.730 --> 01:37:37.980
So maybe one of the reasons
why most people don't

01:37:37.980 --> 01:37:41.170
remember most of their
dreams is for good reason.

01:37:41.170 --> 01:37:43.050
Your hippocampus
is in a state where

01:37:43.050 --> 01:37:44.590
it's not writing new memories.

01:37:44.590 --> 01:37:47.550
In fact, it's writing out
its-- the memories it learned

01:37:47.550 --> 01:37:49.110
during the day to the cortex.

01:37:49.110 --> 01:37:54.270
And it's immune from
incoming new information.

01:37:54.270 --> 01:37:57.960
So maybe lucid dreaming
is bad, because you're

01:37:57.960 --> 01:37:59.730
activating the hippocampus
in a way that's

01:37:59.730 --> 01:38:01.140
writing new memories.

01:38:01.140 --> 01:38:03.090
And it might be
really maladaptive

01:38:03.090 --> 01:38:05.250
for things like PTSD.

01:38:05.250 --> 01:38:09.420
On the other hand, let me just
argue myself right out of this,

01:38:09.420 --> 01:38:12.480
when I used to have a repeated
nightmare when I was a kid,

01:38:12.480 --> 01:38:17.190
my mother who was so wise would
tell me, well, listen, just--

01:38:17.190 --> 01:38:21.510
next time you're in that
dream, say, hey, I'm in a dream

01:38:21.510 --> 01:38:23.170
and then change
something about it.

01:38:23.170 --> 01:38:25.680
So she and I rehearsed
what the horrible

01:38:25.680 --> 01:38:28.710
dream that it was, big
monster running after me.

01:38:28.710 --> 01:38:31.120
And my legs were like mud,
and I couldn't run away.

01:38:31.120 --> 01:38:32.430
And it was just terrifying.

01:38:32.430 --> 01:38:35.610
And that was a dream I would
have time and time again.

01:38:35.610 --> 01:38:37.680
She said, OK, next
time what are you

01:38:37.680 --> 01:38:39.733
going to do when that
monster comes after you?

01:38:39.733 --> 01:38:40.650
I'm going to run away.

01:38:40.650 --> 01:38:42.450
No, that's what
you do every time.

01:38:42.450 --> 01:38:43.860
And it's always
the same outcome.

01:38:43.860 --> 01:38:44.650
You can't run.

01:38:44.650 --> 01:38:46.485
So let's do something different.

01:38:46.485 --> 01:38:48.010
What could you do
that's different?

01:38:48.010 --> 01:38:49.020
So I came up with all--

01:38:49.020 --> 01:38:52.020
I could turn around and
punch it in the nose.

01:38:52.020 --> 01:38:53.100
Yeah, that's great.

01:38:53.100 --> 01:38:55.620
And so the next time
I had that dream--

01:38:55.620 --> 01:38:58.860
I did recognize this is
that same old dream which

01:38:58.860 --> 01:39:00.330
means that there's
part of my brain

01:39:00.330 --> 01:39:04.200
that's conscious enough to know
that I'm in a dreaming state.

01:39:04.200 --> 01:39:07.080
And then I didn't have
the courage in my dream

01:39:07.080 --> 01:39:09.660
because I was still
terrified to punch or touch

01:39:09.660 --> 01:39:10.890
the monster in any way.

01:39:10.890 --> 01:39:12.840
But I did have the
courage to turn around

01:39:12.840 --> 01:39:15.113
and look it in the
eye and say no.

01:39:15.113 --> 01:39:15.780
That was enough.

01:39:15.780 --> 01:39:16.650
I said no.

01:39:16.650 --> 01:39:20.430
And that was enough to knock me
out of that rut of that dream

01:39:20.430 --> 01:39:22.450
so that I never had it again.

01:39:22.450 --> 01:39:24.540
I never had that
same dream again.

01:39:24.540 --> 01:39:27.180
And in fact, it gave me
peace about dreaming,

01:39:27.180 --> 01:39:29.580
because I knew
that if ever there

01:39:29.580 --> 01:39:31.440
was a nightmare that
was just too scary,

01:39:31.440 --> 01:39:35.250
I could probably do something to
change it and knock myself out

01:39:35.250 --> 01:39:35.890
of it.

01:39:35.890 --> 01:39:38.670
So even though, I don't
recommend lucid dreaming

01:39:38.670 --> 01:39:43.530
on a normal day-to-day basis, if
it's enough that can knock you

01:39:43.530 --> 01:39:45.390
out of a rut--

01:39:45.390 --> 01:39:47.430
one thing that happens
with people with PTSD

01:39:47.430 --> 01:39:50.760
is they have the same repeated
horrible nightmare which

01:39:50.760 --> 01:39:55.840
is often a reliving of
the days trauma that had.

01:39:55.840 --> 01:39:59.790
So maybe lucid dreaming
can be used on occasion

01:39:59.790 --> 01:40:04.290
to be a powerful tool, because
there's so much plasticity that

01:40:04.290 --> 01:40:06.780
happens during REM
sleep to knock you out

01:40:06.780 --> 01:40:11.430
of that rut of reliving that
event and just change it.

01:40:11.430 --> 01:40:15.240
And you could probably practice
that during wakefulness,

01:40:15.240 --> 01:40:18.520
rehearse the event that
happened that was so traumatic

01:40:18.520 --> 01:40:23.910
and then just introduce a new
element like, now, I'm safe.

01:40:23.910 --> 01:40:27.210
Now, the sound that was
associated with that really

01:40:27.210 --> 01:40:30.540
traumatic thing, I should now
associate with something else.

01:40:30.540 --> 01:40:32.070
And the next time
I have that dream,

01:40:32.070 --> 01:40:34.350
I'm going to change it
so that sound is now

01:40:34.350 --> 01:40:37.230
this new thing that it should
be associated with safety.

01:40:37.230 --> 01:40:39.390
And that might be
enough, maybe, I

01:40:39.390 --> 01:40:43.560
hope, to knock you out of
that repeated nightmare

01:40:43.560 --> 01:40:45.630
and maybe even start you
on the path to recovery,

01:40:45.630 --> 01:40:48.330
because if you can calm
down about those nightmare

01:40:48.330 --> 01:40:51.360
states of sleep, then maybe
your local coeruleus which

01:40:51.360 --> 01:40:53.670
is involved in stress
can also relax.

01:40:53.670 --> 01:40:56.865
And you can do the erasure
parts that need to be done.

01:40:56.865 --> 01:40:57.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

01:40:57.990 --> 01:41:00.060
I seem to recall a paper--

01:41:00.060 --> 01:41:03.320
and I'll have to find the
reference and send it to you,

01:41:03.320 --> 01:41:05.340
we will also put in the
show note captions--

01:41:05.340 --> 01:41:10.590
that described a protocol
essentially matches this idea.

01:41:10.590 --> 01:41:13.590
And I think what they had people
do was either cue themselves

01:41:13.590 --> 01:41:16.320
to a particular smell
or tone in wakefulness

01:41:16.320 --> 01:41:20.010
then to try and recall
a recurring nightmare.

01:41:20.010 --> 01:41:22.218
Then during the
night sleep, they

01:41:22.218 --> 01:41:24.510
had the tone playing in the
background which would then

01:41:24.510 --> 01:41:26.850
cue them to the wakeful
state-- they're still asleep,

01:41:26.850 --> 01:41:29.430
mind you, but in the pseudo
lucid or lucid state,

01:41:29.430 --> 01:41:33.270
and then try and change some
variable as you're describing.

01:41:33.270 --> 01:41:35.970
Some either look the
predator in the eyes

01:41:35.970 --> 01:41:37.470
or do something different.

01:41:37.470 --> 01:41:40.650
And then in the waking state,
take a little bit of time

01:41:40.650 --> 01:41:44.550
to try and script out a
different narrative altogether.

01:41:44.550 --> 01:41:46.290
And it took several
nights, as I recall,

01:41:46.290 --> 01:41:48.840
or more but that they were
able to escape this recurring

01:41:48.840 --> 01:41:49.340
nightmare.

01:41:49.340 --> 01:41:51.120
GINA POE: It's like a
week or something, yeah.

01:41:51.120 --> 01:41:52.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So you're
familiar with the study?

01:41:52.260 --> 01:41:53.370
GINA POE: That's
a beautiful study.

01:41:53.370 --> 01:41:54.150
I loved it.

01:41:54.150 --> 01:41:56.715
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, we
will put a reference to that.

01:41:56.715 --> 01:41:58.200
I need to revisit that study.

01:41:58.200 --> 01:42:01.320
It was pretty recent, but I
need to dive into it again,

01:42:01.320 --> 01:42:03.990
because I think I didn't go as
deep into it as I should have.

01:42:03.990 --> 01:42:06.992
GINA POE: No, no but the
one thing that you-- you

01:42:06.992 --> 01:42:07.950
said many right things.

01:42:07.950 --> 01:42:09.325
But one of the
things you said is

01:42:09.325 --> 01:42:13.770
that they were able to cue the
dreamer when they knew-- when

01:42:13.770 --> 01:42:15.000
they were going to REM sleep.

01:42:15.000 --> 01:42:17.760
And then they played the
sound or had the odor.

01:42:17.760 --> 01:42:20.157
Now, when you're normally
asleep alone in your bed,

01:42:20.157 --> 01:42:21.990
you're not going to be
able to cue yourself.

01:42:21.990 --> 01:42:25.920
But it might be that rehearsal
enough before you go to sleep

01:42:25.920 --> 01:42:29.820
is enough to help cue you
to that repeated nightmare,

01:42:29.820 --> 01:42:33.360
remembering what the nightmare
is and then figuring out

01:42:33.360 --> 01:42:36.215
how to cue yourself to
do something different.

01:42:36.215 --> 01:42:37.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
For years, I had

01:42:37.590 --> 01:42:41.440
the same recurring nightmare
over, and over, and over again.

01:42:41.440 --> 01:42:43.150
And it was so
salient and so clear.

01:42:43.150 --> 01:42:45.442
And I'm not going to share
what it is, because it's not

01:42:45.442 --> 01:42:46.525
that it's that disturbing.

01:42:46.525 --> 01:42:47.130
It was just--

01:42:47.130 --> 01:42:49.470
I think it was the
emotional load of it

01:42:49.470 --> 01:42:52.050
and just how salient
certain features were,

01:42:52.050 --> 01:42:55.680
like one person who's
a real life person had

01:42:55.680 --> 01:42:59.140
a particular clothing on.

01:42:59.140 --> 01:43:01.140
And it's like, and that
just served as this cue.

01:43:01.140 --> 01:43:03.770
And I don't know if I ever did
any direct work to try and deal

01:43:03.770 --> 01:43:04.270
with it.

01:43:04.270 --> 01:43:06.198
But now, it almost seems
silly to describe it.

01:43:06.198 --> 01:43:06.990
GINA POE: Oh, yeah.

01:43:06.990 --> 01:43:08.782
Well, dreams are usually
silly to describe.

01:43:08.782 --> 01:43:10.157
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It pretty silly.

01:43:10.157 --> 01:43:11.580
But it was a pretty
violent dream.

01:43:11.580 --> 01:43:12.205
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:43:12.205 --> 01:43:14.880
And your emotional
system is so geared

01:43:14.880 --> 01:43:17.220
up during REM sleep
which is another thing we

01:43:17.220 --> 01:43:18.450
could talk about.

01:43:18.450 --> 01:43:19.823
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, please.

01:43:19.823 --> 01:43:20.490
I would love to.

01:43:20.490 --> 01:43:23.430
Yeah, so locus coeruleus
is ideally suppressed.

01:43:23.430 --> 01:43:25.170
So we can't release
norepinephrine.

01:43:25.170 --> 01:43:29.760
We can't act out our dreams
during these very emotionally

01:43:29.760 --> 01:43:34.270
laden thoughts and
storylines during sleep.

01:43:34.270 --> 01:43:37.200
This almost starts to sound
like a little bit of a built

01:43:37.200 --> 01:43:41.130
in while sleeping
trauma therapy,

01:43:41.130 --> 01:43:42.780
because most trauma
therapies involved

01:43:42.780 --> 01:43:45.867
trying to get people
into states of counter

01:43:45.867 --> 01:43:47.450
to what most people
think you actually

01:43:47.450 --> 01:43:50.120
want to get close to the trauma
in terms of the narrative

01:43:50.120 --> 01:43:53.853
or try and suppress the
emotional activity of it or--

01:43:53.853 --> 01:43:56.270
I guess that's the motivation
for ketamine-based therapies

01:43:56.270 --> 01:43:57.050
for trauma--

01:43:57.050 --> 01:43:59.900
or I've also heard, and this
is still perplexing to me,

01:43:59.900 --> 01:44:02.990
that other waking-based
trauma therapies involve

01:44:02.990 --> 01:44:05.240
taking people the other way,
making it very cathartic,

01:44:05.240 --> 01:44:07.520
take them to the peak of
the emotional response

01:44:07.520 --> 01:44:09.920
but then allow that
to finally cycle down

01:44:09.920 --> 01:44:11.640
into a more relaxed response.

01:44:11.640 --> 01:44:16.820
So please, if there's
anything about locus coeruleus

01:44:16.820 --> 01:44:20.990
and dreams and that can help
people basically extinguish

01:44:20.990 --> 01:44:23.877
traumas or traumatic
features to real life events,

01:44:23.877 --> 01:44:25.460
we definitely want
to know about them.

01:44:25.460 --> 01:44:26.085
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:44:26.085 --> 01:44:29.090
Well, I think one of
the things that people

01:44:29.090 --> 01:44:32.480
thought might help after a
trauma like a school shooting

01:44:32.480 --> 01:44:36.860
or whatever, car accident,
is to talk about it.

01:44:36.860 --> 01:44:39.082
But in fact that ended up
being counterproductive.

01:44:39.082 --> 01:44:41.540
And I think one of the reasons
why it was counterproductive

01:44:41.540 --> 01:44:44.990
is because it didn't
take them back down.

01:44:44.990 --> 01:44:47.870
It brought them up and
continued to reactivate

01:44:47.870 --> 01:44:51.050
the emotions of
it but then didn't

01:44:51.050 --> 01:44:54.530
emphasize the safety of
the fact that it's over

01:44:54.530 --> 01:44:58.010
or help them work through
how they might avoid it again

01:44:58.010 --> 01:45:02.000
in the future to calm the
sympathetic nervous system down

01:45:02.000 --> 01:45:03.620
again before they went to sleep.

01:45:03.620 --> 01:45:06.860
And none of these studies has
sleep ever been considered.

01:45:06.860 --> 01:45:10.760
But to me, that's the key
part, is bringing down

01:45:10.760 --> 01:45:12.590
your sympathetic nervous
system before you

01:45:12.590 --> 01:45:15.080
go to sleep so that your
sleep can be adaptive,

01:45:15.080 --> 01:45:17.420
your locus release can
shut off like it normally

01:45:17.420 --> 01:45:23.370
does or should do and then able
to erase the novelty of it.

01:45:23.370 --> 01:45:27.200
The other thing that I
just mentioned a minute ago

01:45:27.200 --> 01:45:30.410
was that the emotional system is
highly activated in REM sleep.

01:45:30.410 --> 01:45:31.980
And that's definitely true.

01:45:31.980 --> 01:45:34.070
And that might seem
counterproductive

01:45:34.070 --> 01:45:37.520
in terms of the
nightmares and how

01:45:37.520 --> 01:45:41.660
to help REM sleep be
a therapeutic thing

01:45:41.660 --> 01:45:47.900
rather than reinforcing the
emotionality of the trauma.

01:45:47.900 --> 01:45:50.540
And I think the key
to that again is

01:45:50.540 --> 01:45:52.350
the absence of norepinephrine.

01:45:52.350 --> 01:45:56.930
So even though the emotional
system is in high gear,

01:45:56.930 --> 01:46:02.150
without norepinephrine,
you can actually

01:46:02.150 --> 01:46:05.990
divorce those highly
activated emotions

01:46:05.990 --> 01:46:09.650
from the cognitive
parts of the memory

01:46:09.650 --> 01:46:13.040
that you have just written
out in that N2 stage of sleep

01:46:13.040 --> 01:46:16.850
when the sleep
spindles are going.

01:46:16.850 --> 01:46:19.730
So you've just now
consolidated the information

01:46:19.730 --> 01:46:23.360
that you'll need to survive
and to make that adaptive.

01:46:23.360 --> 01:46:27.410
And now, you need to
divorce from that schema

01:46:27.410 --> 01:46:31.370
and from that semantic parts
of memory, the emotional part,

01:46:31.370 --> 01:46:33.860
because whenever you
remember something,

01:46:33.860 --> 01:46:37.970
it's fine if you remember the
being emotional at the time.

01:46:37.970 --> 01:46:41.270
But you don't want to bring
back and sew into that memory

01:46:41.270 --> 01:46:42.920
all of the same
emotional systems.

01:46:42.920 --> 01:46:45.590
You don't want to bring
back the heart rate

01:46:45.590 --> 01:46:48.530
changes, and the
sweating, and all of that.

01:46:48.530 --> 01:46:51.110
You want to be able to
remember all the parts of it

01:46:51.110 --> 01:46:53.390
and even remember that
you were traumatized

01:46:53.390 --> 01:46:56.525
and that you did cry
and that you did have--

01:46:56.525 --> 01:46:57.740
your heart was racing.

01:46:57.740 --> 01:47:00.740
But when you're talking
about it years later,

01:47:00.740 --> 01:47:03.120
you don't want to have to
relive all that, otherwise who

01:47:03.120 --> 01:47:06.477
would ever want to recall a
traumatic memory because you're

01:47:06.477 --> 01:47:08.810
basically putting yourself
through the same trauma which

01:47:08.810 --> 01:47:10.910
is what people with PTSD have.

01:47:10.910 --> 01:47:13.460
They don't want to recall
this traumatic memory,

01:47:13.460 --> 01:47:15.140
because it's reliving it.

01:47:15.140 --> 01:47:16.920
Like it's just happening again.

01:47:16.920 --> 01:47:21.890
So that's what we're thinking
is that the emotional parts are

01:47:21.890 --> 01:47:24.440
no longer-- are not
able to be divorced

01:47:24.440 --> 01:47:27.260
because the norepinephrine
system is not

01:47:27.260 --> 01:47:28.820
downscaled during REM sleep.

01:47:28.820 --> 01:47:33.950
And so that REM sleep serves to
instead reinforce and in fact

01:47:33.950 --> 01:47:37.970
amplify the emotions because
your emotional system is up,

01:47:37.970 --> 01:47:41.120
locus coeruleus
is high, re-sewing

01:47:41.120 --> 01:47:44.630
in every night the
emotionality of those memories

01:47:44.630 --> 01:47:46.790
and with the memory itself.

01:47:46.790 --> 01:47:50.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You've told
us a lot about locus coeruleus

01:47:50.450 --> 01:47:52.160
and norepinephrine
from locus coeruleus.

01:47:52.160 --> 01:47:56.910
Is there any role for
norepinephrine, epinephrine,

01:47:56.910 --> 01:47:59.670
and cortisol released
from the adrenals?

01:47:59.670 --> 01:48:04.200
My understanding is that
norepinephrine and epinephrine

01:48:04.200 --> 01:48:06.750
will not cross the blood brain
barrier which is probably

01:48:06.750 --> 01:48:10.320
why we have a brain-based
noradrenergic system,

01:48:10.320 --> 01:48:12.810
locus coeruleus,
and other neurons.

01:48:12.810 --> 01:48:14.950
Actually, that's a
question I should ask you.

01:48:14.950 --> 01:48:16.050
Are there other
sites in the brain

01:48:16.050 --> 01:48:17.592
where norepinephrine
is released from

01:48:17.592 --> 01:48:19.290
or is it just locus coeruleus?

01:48:19.290 --> 01:48:23.940
GINA POE: So there are seven,
nine different adrenergic.

01:48:23.940 --> 01:48:26.813
Yes, there's nine different
adrenergic structures.

01:48:26.813 --> 01:48:28.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm
sorry I didn't ask.

01:48:28.480 --> 01:48:32.730
But it just occurred to me that
in some cases like with raphe,

01:48:32.730 --> 01:48:35.518
there are other sources
of serotonergic drive

01:48:35.518 --> 01:48:36.060
in the brain.

01:48:36.060 --> 01:48:38.370
But raphe is like the main side.

01:48:38.370 --> 01:48:41.090
GINA POE: Yeah, that's the
one that goes to the cortex

01:48:41.090 --> 01:48:42.960
and the locus coeruleus
is also the one

01:48:42.960 --> 01:48:44.190
that goes to the cortex.

01:48:44.190 --> 01:48:46.800
But there are other adrenergic
sources, some that--

01:48:46.800 --> 01:48:50.460
from the brainstem that descend
and help us to ignore pain,

01:48:50.460 --> 01:48:54.180
for example, when we're
stressed and needing to run away

01:48:54.180 --> 01:48:55.298
from the tiger, right?

01:48:55.298 --> 01:48:57.340
We don't want to be
thinking, oh, my ankle hurts.

01:48:57.340 --> 01:48:58.882
You want to just be
able to ignore it

01:48:58.882 --> 01:49:00.450
and go do what you need to do.

01:49:00.450 --> 01:49:05.820
So yeah, so there are lots of
other noradrenergic nuclei.

01:49:05.820 --> 01:49:07.800
But the locus coeruleus
is the main one that

01:49:07.800 --> 01:49:09.135
projects all over the brain.

01:49:09.135 --> 01:49:11.010
Actually, the only place
that doesn't project

01:49:11.010 --> 01:49:12.540
is the dorsal striatum.

01:49:12.540 --> 01:49:15.130
You talked about ventral
striatum and addiction.

01:49:15.130 --> 01:49:16.890
The dorsal striatum
is the only place

01:49:16.890 --> 01:49:19.230
the locus coeruleus
doesn't project to.

01:49:19.230 --> 01:49:22.290
And that's involved in
procedural learning and motor

01:49:22.290 --> 01:49:25.260
learning, kinds of
learning that take over

01:49:25.260 --> 01:49:28.173
when your hippocampus, for
example, is compromised.

01:49:28.173 --> 01:49:30.090
Bilateral, if you don't
have good hippocampus,

01:49:30.090 --> 01:49:32.280
you can still do procedural
learning and do--

01:49:32.280 --> 01:49:33.000
and it's great.

01:49:33.000 --> 01:49:34.540
It's a redundant system.

01:49:34.540 --> 01:49:37.500
And so if your locus
coeruleus is not

01:49:37.500 --> 01:49:40.608
working, if you don't have it
anymore, you can still do a--

01:49:40.608 --> 01:49:42.150
if you don't have
a good hippocampus,

01:49:42.150 --> 01:49:44.370
you can still do learning
through this dorsal striatum

01:49:44.370 --> 01:49:45.090
on the structure.

01:49:45.090 --> 01:49:50.820
So it might be for those
kinds of learning functions,

01:49:50.820 --> 01:49:52.560
sleep deprivation
where you never

01:49:52.560 --> 01:49:55.740
let the locus coeruleus
stop firing is OK,

01:49:55.740 --> 01:49:59.050
because it doesn't have any
receptors for norepinephrine

01:49:59.050 --> 01:49:59.550
anyway.

01:49:59.550 --> 01:50:01.080
So yeah.

01:50:01.080 --> 01:50:03.645
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And what
about bodily like adrenals.

01:50:06.210 --> 01:50:09.120
I often remind people there's no
such thing as adrenal burnout,

01:50:09.120 --> 01:50:13.110
per se, that adrenals
don't actually burn out.

01:50:13.110 --> 01:50:16.170
But some people have adrenal
insufficiency syndrome.

01:50:16.170 --> 01:50:18.060
Other people have
adrenals that are just

01:50:18.060 --> 01:50:22.320
chronically cranking out
epinephrine, norepinephrine,

01:50:22.320 --> 01:50:25.470
and cortisol at the wrong
times in particular.

01:50:25.470 --> 01:50:26.790
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:50:26.790 --> 01:50:30.150
So that-- those are
great questions.

01:50:30.150 --> 01:50:32.610
And I think the answers
to them have yet

01:50:32.610 --> 01:50:34.050
to be discovered,
the connections

01:50:34.050 --> 01:50:36.820
between our periphery and
our central nervous system.

01:50:36.820 --> 01:50:39.120
But we know that there
are beautiful connections.

01:50:39.120 --> 01:50:42.270
And it's untapped
source of being

01:50:42.270 --> 01:50:45.780
able to manipulate our brains
is to work through our bodies.

01:50:45.780 --> 01:50:49.320
And so our adrenals
do great things.

01:50:49.320 --> 01:50:51.190
They constrict
our blood vessels,

01:50:51.190 --> 01:50:54.420
causing higher blood pressure
which helps blood rush out

01:50:54.420 --> 01:50:59.280
to all the extremities that need
blood, our muscles for example,

01:50:59.280 --> 01:51:03.087
for running away from
the lion, or the tiger--

01:51:03.087 --> 01:51:04.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Or
meeting a grant deadline

01:51:04.920 --> 01:51:07.260
or catching a train.

01:51:07.260 --> 01:51:09.090
GINA POE: Or catching
a train, yeah.

01:51:09.090 --> 01:51:11.460
The adrenals help our
hearts pump faster.

01:51:11.460 --> 01:51:16.140
Our muscles get perfused
with the blood it needs.

01:51:16.140 --> 01:51:18.600
It diverts blood
and everything away

01:51:18.600 --> 01:51:22.840
from our parasympathetic system
which is rest and digest.

01:51:22.840 --> 01:51:24.898
We don't really need to
digest that croissant

01:51:24.898 --> 01:51:26.190
when we're running for a train.

01:51:26.190 --> 01:51:28.180
We can do that later.

01:51:28.180 --> 01:51:31.490
So it's doing really
important things.

01:51:31.490 --> 01:51:33.990
What we don't know, because it
doesn't cross the blood brain

01:51:33.990 --> 01:51:37.800
barrier, is how that affects
the brain and whether our--

01:51:37.800 --> 01:51:43.500
if we can independently activate
our adrenals, when a time when

01:51:43.500 --> 01:51:47.520
our brain thinks that we should
be fine and calm and asleep,

01:51:47.520 --> 01:51:50.100
how our brain detects that.

01:51:50.100 --> 01:51:52.680
Is it a feedback
through heart is racing?

01:51:52.680 --> 01:51:54.900
And then our brainstem
says, what's going on,

01:51:54.900 --> 01:51:57.150
my heart is racing,
and then wakes us up.

01:51:57.150 --> 01:52:00.900
And then our hearts were racing
together with our brain racing.

01:52:00.900 --> 01:52:04.110
We just don't know the answers
to these questions yet.

01:52:04.110 --> 01:52:07.350
There are some good
studies, old studies.

01:52:07.350 --> 01:52:10.020
But we need a lot more.

01:52:10.020 --> 01:52:12.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I will--
another nod to the fact

01:52:12.120 --> 01:52:15.407
that there's lots of great
work ongoing and still to do.

01:52:15.407 --> 01:52:17.490
I'd love for you to tell
us about some of the work

01:52:17.490 --> 01:52:19.615
that you're doing more
recently on the relationship

01:52:19.615 --> 01:52:24.900
between sleep and
opiate use, withdrawal

01:52:24.900 --> 01:52:29.760
relapse, and craving,
just addiction, generally.

01:52:29.760 --> 01:52:32.820
I get a lot of questions
about people trying

01:52:32.820 --> 01:52:36.900
to come off benzodiazepines
or people's challenges

01:52:36.900 --> 01:52:41.190
with benzodiazepine and
other types of addiction.

01:52:41.190 --> 01:52:44.100
What is the role of sleep
in addiction and recovery

01:52:44.100 --> 01:52:46.200
from addiction and
opiates in particular?

01:52:46.200 --> 01:52:47.010
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:52:47.010 --> 01:52:49.170
This is a very young area.

01:52:49.170 --> 01:52:51.330
And in fact, my laboratory
has just started.

01:52:51.330 --> 01:52:54.030
I have a graduate student
who's been in my lab

01:52:54.030 --> 01:52:54.900
for just one year.

01:52:54.900 --> 01:52:59.110
She's done amazing work already
but completely groundbreaking

01:52:59.110 --> 01:52:59.610
work.

01:52:59.610 --> 01:53:01.800
And what she has
discovered already--

01:53:01.800 --> 01:53:04.830
we don't have the paper out
yet, but we're working on it--

01:53:04.830 --> 01:53:09.690
is that when animals
withdraw from opiates--

01:53:09.690 --> 01:53:12.510
and this has been
replicated in other ways

01:53:12.510 --> 01:53:15.030
with other types of things--

01:53:15.030 --> 01:53:16.410
our sleep is disturbed.

01:53:16.410 --> 01:53:18.000
Our sleep is terribly disturbed.

01:53:18.000 --> 01:53:19.890
And the amount of
sleep disturbance

01:53:19.890 --> 01:53:22.620
predicts relapse behaviors.

01:53:22.620 --> 01:53:24.300
And you might think,
well, of course,

01:53:24.300 --> 01:53:26.070
you're going to relapse
if you can't sleep

01:53:26.070 --> 01:53:27.480
because opiates calm you down.

01:53:27.480 --> 01:53:29.772
Well, then one of the reasons
why opiates calm you down

01:53:29.772 --> 01:53:32.160
is because the locus coeruleus,
again, the blue spot,

01:53:32.160 --> 01:53:35.610
is covered with
opiate receptors that

01:53:35.610 --> 01:53:39.600
are normally really responsive
to our endogenous opiates.

01:53:39.600 --> 01:53:43.380
And so what happens
when we are pleased,

01:53:43.380 --> 01:53:45.570
for example, or
laughing or whatever,

01:53:45.570 --> 01:53:48.570
endogenous dodges
opiates activate

01:53:48.570 --> 01:53:51.000
those receptors in the locus
coeruleus and calm it down.

01:53:51.000 --> 01:53:55.080
It actually suppresses
locus coeruleus activity.

01:53:55.080 --> 01:53:57.000
It makes us happy and relaxed.

01:53:57.000 --> 01:53:59.610
One of the things, reasons
why opiates are so addictive

01:53:59.610 --> 01:54:03.900
is because it also calms us
down and makes us relaxed.

01:54:03.900 --> 01:54:06.480
But the problem with
exogenous opiates

01:54:06.480 --> 01:54:10.680
is that they really strongly
bind these receptors

01:54:10.680 --> 01:54:11.950
on our locus coeruleus.

01:54:11.950 --> 01:54:15.268
And if you take exogenous
opiates again and again

01:54:15.268 --> 01:54:17.310
like you're recovering
from surgery, for example,

01:54:17.310 --> 01:54:19.440
and take these
pain medications is

01:54:19.440 --> 01:54:21.090
that our locus
coeruleus struggles

01:54:21.090 --> 01:54:23.940
to do what it's supposed to
do which is keep us awake,

01:54:23.940 --> 01:54:26.220
and learning, and
concentrating on things.

01:54:26.220 --> 01:54:27.960
So it will down regulate.

01:54:27.960 --> 01:54:30.030
It will internalize
these receptors

01:54:30.030 --> 01:54:33.060
that are normally only
occupied by endogenous opiates.

01:54:33.060 --> 01:54:35.490
And it will do
this-- it will change

01:54:35.490 --> 01:54:38.920
our genes that are associated
with producing these receptors.

01:54:38.920 --> 01:54:41.333
So you actually have very
many fewer receptors.

01:54:41.333 --> 01:54:43.500
So the locus coeruleus, at
least, during wakefulness

01:54:43.500 --> 01:54:46.650
can fire and to help us
to do these things learn

01:54:46.650 --> 01:54:48.160
about our environment.

01:54:48.160 --> 01:54:51.600
And so if you long term
reduce the number of receptors

01:54:51.600 --> 01:54:55.140
out there, then when you
withdraw the exogenous opiate,

01:54:55.140 --> 01:54:57.360
there is not enough
of your endogenous

01:54:57.360 --> 01:55:02.410
opiates to be able to occupy
those few receptors that

01:55:02.410 --> 01:55:02.910
are there.

01:55:02.910 --> 01:55:05.430
And our locus has nothing
to calm it down anymore,

01:55:05.430 --> 01:55:06.480
no pacifier.

01:55:06.480 --> 01:55:08.850
And it just fires,
and fires, and fires.

01:55:08.850 --> 01:55:13.350
And that physic and
tonic high activity

01:55:13.350 --> 01:55:15.570
stresses us out,
because it's normally

01:55:15.570 --> 01:55:17.130
associated with stress.

01:55:17.130 --> 01:55:20.160
And so any exogenous
stressor that adds to that

01:55:20.160 --> 01:55:22.440
and also activates
our locus coeruleus,

01:55:22.440 --> 01:55:24.610
there's nothing to
calm it down again.

01:55:24.610 --> 01:55:26.400
And so it just keeps firing.

01:55:26.400 --> 01:55:28.350
It disturbs our sleep.

01:55:28.350 --> 01:55:30.720
And that's why maybe
sleep disturbance

01:55:30.720 --> 01:55:35.580
is an indicator of
hyperactive locus coeruleus

01:55:35.580 --> 01:55:39.930
and such a good
predictor of relapse

01:55:39.930 --> 01:55:43.200
behaviors because nobody likes
to live in that high stress

01:55:43.200 --> 01:55:43.890
state.

01:55:43.890 --> 01:55:48.040
And they will do anything
to get back to normal.

01:55:48.040 --> 01:55:52.680
So the problem with
taking these drugs

01:55:52.680 --> 01:55:56.930
is that it leaves you excited--

01:55:56.930 --> 01:55:59.495
I'm sorry, excited,
relaxed and happy.

01:55:59.495 --> 01:56:00.870
But then when you
come off of it,

01:56:00.870 --> 01:56:04.170
you're worse than when
you were at baseline.

01:56:04.170 --> 01:56:05.920
You take it again.

01:56:05.920 --> 01:56:07.800
It only brings you up
this far, because you

01:56:07.800 --> 01:56:08.910
have fewer receptors.

01:56:08.910 --> 01:56:12.090
When you come off it, you're
down, even more depressed

01:56:12.090 --> 01:56:14.190
and anxious and--

01:56:14.190 --> 01:56:16.320
depressed is a
word I use loosely,

01:56:16.320 --> 01:56:17.610
and that's not what I--

01:56:17.610 --> 01:56:19.693
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Certainly,
central nervous system

01:56:19.693 --> 01:56:23.160
depression, sleepier, less
motivated, lower mood.

01:56:23.160 --> 01:56:23.910
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:56:23.910 --> 01:56:26.025
Our locus coeruleus
is actually--

01:56:26.025 --> 01:56:28.650
it's a anxiety kind of
depression actually,

01:56:28.650 --> 01:56:30.090
the anxiety related depression.

01:56:30.090 --> 01:56:32.310
So yeah.

01:56:32.310 --> 01:56:34.725
So we don't know yet what--

01:56:34.725 --> 01:56:36.850
and there's some good
research going on right now--

01:56:36.850 --> 01:56:40.950
what could restore our own
endogenous receptors so

01:56:40.950 --> 01:56:44.610
that our endogenous opiates
can properly calm our locus

01:56:44.610 --> 01:56:48.240
coeruleus once that they
have been tamped down

01:56:48.240 --> 01:56:49.590
by exogenous opiates.

01:56:49.590 --> 01:56:53.530
But that would be really one way
that you can access the sleep

01:56:53.530 --> 01:56:54.030
disturbance.

01:56:54.030 --> 01:56:56.362
So we talked about sleep
and the importance of sleep

01:56:56.362 --> 01:56:58.320
in terms of learning and
memory, the importance

01:56:58.320 --> 01:57:01.140
of the structure of the
90-minute cycle for all

01:57:01.140 --> 01:57:01.780
of that.

01:57:01.780 --> 01:57:04.380
So you can imagine if your
sleep is disturbed by too much

01:57:04.380 --> 01:57:07.305
locus coeruleus activity.

01:57:07.305 --> 01:57:10.920
The structure and the function
of those sleep spindles

01:57:10.920 --> 01:57:14.850
and that theta during REM sleep
and the lack of norepinephrine,

01:57:14.850 --> 01:57:20.220
all of those structures, all
those functions for learning

01:57:20.220 --> 01:57:24.060
something new, like a new
behavior that doesn't involve

01:57:24.060 --> 01:57:26.940
the drugs becomes compromised.

01:57:26.940 --> 01:57:30.180
And so that's something that
Tania Lugo, in collaboration

01:57:30.180 --> 01:57:33.270
with Pamela Kennedy
at UCLA, that we're

01:57:33.270 --> 01:57:36.150
looking at how is learning and
memory affected by the sleep

01:57:36.150 --> 01:57:36.750
disturbance.

01:57:36.750 --> 01:57:38.850
If there's a way we can--

01:57:38.850 --> 01:57:41.010
in animals that are
coming off of opiates.

01:57:41.010 --> 01:57:43.320
Can we restore their
sleep to normal

01:57:43.320 --> 01:57:47.130
so that then they
are less likely to do

01:57:47.130 --> 01:57:49.462
relapse kinds of behaviors.

01:57:49.462 --> 01:57:50.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fascinating.

01:57:50.670 --> 01:57:54.242
And I will certainly have to
have you back on to tell us

01:57:54.242 --> 01:57:55.450
the results of those studies.

01:57:55.450 --> 01:57:57.130
Meanwhile, I think
for anyone who's

01:57:57.130 --> 01:58:00.790
trying to come off
opiates, exogenous opiates

01:58:00.790 --> 01:58:03.250
and restore these
systems, what I'm hearing

01:58:03.250 --> 01:58:06.490
is that it's going to take
some time but that any and all

01:58:06.490 --> 01:58:08.380
things that people
can do to buffer

01:58:08.380 --> 01:58:11.800
their healthy normal sleep
architecture like morning

01:58:11.800 --> 01:58:14.170
and daytime sunlight, limiting
bright light exposure,

01:58:14.170 --> 01:58:16.378
lowering the temperature at
night, a number of things

01:58:16.378 --> 01:58:18.190
that we've talked
about in this podcast--

01:58:18.190 --> 01:58:20.465
GINA POE: Breathing
exercises, meditation,

01:58:20.465 --> 01:58:22.840
whatever it is that helps you
calm yourself before sleep,

01:58:22.840 --> 01:58:23.140
yeah.

01:58:23.140 --> 01:58:24.098
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:58:24.098 --> 01:58:27.160
Would facilitate not just sleep
but perhaps even accelerate

01:58:27.160 --> 01:58:30.460
the recovery and
shorten this period

01:58:30.460 --> 01:58:32.920
of withdrawal, which
from the questions I get

01:58:32.920 --> 01:58:36.640
and from what I hear can
be absolutely brutal.

01:58:36.640 --> 01:58:37.300
GINA POE: Yeah.

01:58:37.300 --> 01:58:38.530
Oh, I can imagine.

01:58:38.530 --> 01:58:40.990
I had to take opiates for--

01:58:40.990 --> 01:58:43.450
I only took it for three
days after giving birth

01:58:43.450 --> 01:58:48.832
to my first son, I think,
second son, one of them.

01:58:48.832 --> 01:58:51.610
And just-- I just said after
three days, this is enough.

01:58:51.610 --> 01:58:53.110
I'm just going to try Tylenol.

01:58:53.110 --> 01:58:56.410
And so I weaned
myself-- not weaned,

01:58:56.410 --> 01:58:58.420
I just did a sudden
sharp cut off.

01:58:58.420 --> 01:59:00.430
And even though I felt--

01:59:00.430 --> 01:59:02.980
I didn't get the high
of opiates when I

01:59:02.980 --> 01:59:07.480
was taking the Tylenol codeine.

01:59:07.480 --> 01:59:11.140
When I went off it, boy,
it was like PMS times 100.

01:59:11.140 --> 01:59:15.250
I was so anxious and
upset at little things.

01:59:15.250 --> 01:59:17.060
And thankfully, it only
lasted a few hours.

01:59:17.060 --> 01:59:19.510
But if I had taken it
for a week or two weeks,

01:59:19.510 --> 01:59:23.200
who knows if my endogenous
opiate receptors would

01:59:23.200 --> 01:59:24.940
have been permanently
downregulated

01:59:24.940 --> 01:59:27.370
and I would have
been an addict--

01:59:27.370 --> 01:59:29.200
or an addict-- I would
have been addicted.

01:59:29.200 --> 01:59:30.490
I shouldn't say an addict.

01:59:30.490 --> 01:59:31.900
There's negative connotations.

01:59:31.900 --> 01:59:33.700
It's just a very
physiological state.

01:59:33.700 --> 01:59:37.340
So no judgments at all
associated with it.

01:59:37.340 --> 01:59:41.290
So yeah, They're powerful,
powerful painkillers

01:59:41.290 --> 01:59:45.220
but can also alter your
entire brain and rewire it.

01:59:45.220 --> 01:59:47.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
all the more reason

01:59:47.110 --> 01:59:49.027
why I and many others
are grateful that you're

01:59:49.027 --> 01:59:50.680
doing this work
to figure out ways

01:59:50.680 --> 01:59:55.880
that people can recover more
quickly and more thoroughly.

01:59:55.880 --> 01:59:59.510
I must say you've taught
us a tremendous amount

01:59:59.510 --> 02:00:01.730
in a relatively
short amount of time

02:00:01.730 --> 02:00:04.550
about the architecture of
sleep, the different phases,

02:00:04.550 --> 02:00:06.560
the relationship between
sleep and dreaming

02:00:06.560 --> 02:00:08.600
and this incredible
structure, locus coeruleus.

02:00:08.600 --> 02:00:10.550
And I'm so happy we
also got into the pons.

02:00:10.550 --> 02:00:13.430
That just delights me,
because we rarely talk

02:00:13.430 --> 02:00:14.870
about the pons on this podcast.

02:00:14.870 --> 02:00:16.820
It's such an
interesting structure.

02:00:16.820 --> 02:00:19.790
Sex differences that are
important in creativity

02:00:19.790 --> 02:00:23.690
and problem solving and trauma
sleep spindles, just such

02:00:23.690 --> 02:00:25.070
a wealth of information.

02:00:25.070 --> 02:00:27.410
And much of it that's
actionable for people.

02:00:27.410 --> 02:00:29.223
So first of all, I
want to say thank you

02:00:29.223 --> 02:00:30.890
for taking the time
to sit down and have

02:00:30.890 --> 02:00:32.900
this conversation that
so many people are

02:00:32.900 --> 02:00:33.805
sure to benefit from.

02:00:33.805 --> 02:00:35.930
I also want to thank you
for doing the work you do.

02:00:35.930 --> 02:00:38.130
Even though I'm a
fellow neurobiologist,

02:00:38.130 --> 02:00:43.640
I think that it's not often that
we take a step back and realize

02:00:43.640 --> 02:00:47.930
that it's really
the work of hard--

02:00:47.930 --> 02:00:52.940
thinking hard, strongly
motivated PIs--

02:00:52.940 --> 02:00:54.530
it stands for
principal investigator

02:00:54.530 --> 02:00:56.630
by the way, PIs like
yourself, graduate students,

02:00:56.630 --> 02:00:58.910
and post-docs that really
drive the discovery forward

02:00:58.910 --> 02:01:01.070
and that lead to these
new therapeutics.

02:01:01.070 --> 02:01:02.330
Physicians are wonderful.

02:01:02.330 --> 02:01:04.050
Clinicians are
absolutely wonderful.

02:01:04.050 --> 02:01:06.260
But clinicians don't
develop new treatments.

02:01:06.260 --> 02:01:08.900
They only implement the ones
that researchers discover.

02:01:08.900 --> 02:01:12.620
So thank you for
being a brain explorer

02:01:12.620 --> 02:01:16.650
with a focus on growing
the good in the world.

02:01:16.650 --> 02:01:20.210
I know I speak for everybody
when I say thank you so much.

02:01:20.210 --> 02:01:21.710
GINA POE: Thank you
so much, Andrew.

02:01:21.710 --> 02:01:23.720
Thank you for being an
amazing interviewer.

02:01:23.720 --> 02:01:26.780
You brought a lot out of
me in a coherent fashion

02:01:26.780 --> 02:01:29.450
that normally I can't do
when I'm speaking in public.

02:01:29.450 --> 02:01:30.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
don't know about that.

02:01:30.140 --> 02:01:31.730
I've heard your lectures,
and they're superb.

02:01:31.730 --> 02:01:33.470
We'll direct people to
some of the other ones.

02:01:33.470 --> 02:01:34.290
GINA POE: Well, thank you.

02:01:34.290 --> 02:01:36.620
And I also want to put a
plug-in for graduate students

02:01:36.620 --> 02:01:39.380
in general and the
key and amazing role

02:01:39.380 --> 02:01:41.930
